INSERT INTO posts (title, slug, content, excerpt, image_url, categories, tags, author, wp_id, published, created_at, updated_at)
VALUES ('Communities to play responsible roles as virus spreads', 'communities-to-play-responsible-roles-as-virus-spreads', 'Editor, <strong>Allen Meagher,</strong> asked <strong>Pat Kennedy</strong> of <a href="https://etownz.ie">eTownz</a> to give advice on how communities can best prepare, cope and communicate with the impact of coronavirus. Our editorial team reviewed the article and recommend it highly. We would like to hear from anyone else with a long-range view of how communities in Ireland can manage in the face of uncertainty. <strong>This is Pat''s article:</strong>

<strong>The first thing to do is to form a local team of relevant stakeholders:</strong>
The local residents council, development team or similar grouping should consider forming a team of relevant stakeholders. The team should be kept relatively small (5-15 people). It could for example include local GPs, school principals, business leaders and community leaders and people who help with community communications e.g. those who run community websites and social media.

The group doesn’t have to physically meet. They could meet via Skype, Google hangouts or Zoom for example.
Skype: If lots of your people already use Skype, stick with Skype. It is very popular and may be the simplest for online meetings and discussions.
Google Hangouts: If you’re well-organised and people are comfortable with technology, Google Hangouts is great. There are lots of cool tools with it: You can share screens on it, draw on it, create tasks and so on, although it can be glitchy.
Zoom: This is new, but quick and easy. It’s become very popular in recent years. If starting from scratch, I’d go with Zoom.

<strong>This team can achieve much:</strong>

1. Educate &amp; disseminate!
• Disseminate official updates, warnings, training and instructions to relevant players in the community. You can do this by printing leaflets, through social media or the local newspaper, or all three.
• People involved in education may require slightly different instructions to those in health care or business owners and so on.
• The HSE and other organisations are providing public health notices and they will get more frequent and specialised if the disease spreads further with more specific actions for communities where the virus is present.
<h3>2. Represent &amp; organise!</h3>
• The local health service or similar bodies may request communities to help take certain actions such as to set up disinfection centres or to reduce/stop social gatherings for a period of time.
• If a number of people in the community become infected, there maybe be the need to request people to reduce interaction with others - which creates challenges for accessing normal community services, shopping, socialising, work, metc.
• There also may be a need to ensure everyone in the community is in touch, and that isolated or vulnerable people in the community can be checked on by phone or email regularly.
<h3>3. Publish trustworthy communications!</h3>
• With the need to reduce human to human contact, digital communications are going to become very important and in particular social media management.
• Communities will respond positively to clearly defined communications, given in a trusted manner, with a consistent message.
• The HSE could find it really useful if there is a means to talk to a community directly. Your locally-trusted and dedicated team will be in a pivotal place to assist.
• It’s worth saying that the managers of local Facebook community pages could very quickly have very important roles. Likewise for pages run by local authorities.
• The social media managers of these pages should be given guidelines and ideally training. If things get worse, people will go to these pages to communicate. They may have to manage situations where people get disgruntled or hysterical, while they need to remain calm and clear to communicate the HSE’s messages.

<em>Pat’s previous article is also of some relevance (p25, Issue 66, Autumn 2019). You can read ‘Smart Community Planning &amp; Management for Extreme Weather Events’ at: www.changingireland.ie/digital-magazine-archive/</em>', 'Doctors in Italy warn that a tsunami is coming. Here, we look at the roles communities can play in appropriately dealing with and being as resilient as possible as Coronavirus spreads.', '/files/uploads/2020/03/hold-virtual-meetings-copy-copy.jpg', '[{"slug":"development-work","title":"Development Work"},{"slug":"editors-picks","title":"Editor''s Picks"},{"slug":"planning-governance","title":"Planning & Governance"}]', '[{"slug":"changingireland","title":"#ChangingIreland"},{"slug":"changing-ireland","title":"Changing Ireland"},{"slug":"community-development","title":"community development"},{"slug":"community-facilities","title":"community facilities"},{"slug":"coronavirus","title":"coronavirus"},{"slug":"opinion","title":"Opinion"},{"slug":"technology","title":"technology"}]', 'Pat Kennedy', 248878, 0, '2020-03-10T14:02:50Z', '2020-03-10T14:02:50Z')
