INSERT INTO posts (title, slug, content, excerpt, image_url, categories, tags, author, wp_id, published, created_at, updated_at)
VALUES ('Harry’s Diary – Day 41 (Fri, April 24, 2020)', 'harrys-diary-day-41-fri-april-24-2020', 'Everyday feels like Saturday to me. Which is good. Imagine a life where everyday has that Saturday morning, eat rashers for breakfast and do whatever you want to do, feeling! Oh wait, I’m living it. I should probably wash the car.

I’m honestly not sure is it Thursday or Friday? I don’t care. I’m pretty sure the hunter-gatherers didn’t name the days and cycle their life accordingly. I would prefer to be a hunter-gatherer.

“But you’d die of a broken leg,” I have heard in response.

I don’t care - a short life of Saturdays beats a long one of Mondays or any weekday.

It’s a challenge to write today. The event that has affected me more than any other this year is a private affair. I’m a different person today than I was yesterday. Well, maybe not different, but I certainly view myself differently.

I want my writing to explore the truth, all of it, the darkest depths. It turns out there are limits. It’s why I decided to explore fiction work. I have mentioned it before here. I suppose that is what art is for. I’ll find a way of expressing it in a respectful way as time goes by.

<em>Journalists don’t care. It’s strange to have lived on both sides of the fence.</em>

<em>One was on scene within minutes. I’d love to know his source. I hope he was happy with the photos he took with his extra-long zoom from what seemed like a kilometre away. Maybe there should just be an official wire sent out from the emergency services and leave it that.</em>

<em>It’s bizarre that monumental trauma for one can be condensed to a few short lines, barely an afterthought perhaps, by another. It’s just the way the world works. Humans aren’t capable of empathy. We just think we are because we like to pretend we are gods.</em>

<em>Life is experiential. It happens in your moment and your moment alone. If I get cut, you don’t bleed. When we claim empathy what we really mean is imagination informed by our own experiences.</em>

<em>“Wow, you must be feeling exactly like I would feel if I was in that situation that I wasn’t?”</em>

<em>“Nah, I’m good, I don’t ‘must feel’ anything you think I ought, from your different frame of reference - thanks”</em>

<em>After all the years of counselling and talking and ruminating and reading and praying and drinking I’ve realised something - I can’t even empathise with myself. That becomes really clear when you write.</em>

<em>Experiences from the past become condensed into a feeling that is evoked when you try and recall them. There are maybe a few stand-out quotes, the ‘he-said / she-saids’ that the ego edits to validate feelings and the decisions made from them. Try writing it though. The smells, the sounds, the second-by-second thoughts. It’s impossible, at best the barest reflection in a dirty window.</em>

<em>Which is great: The story of myself is just a myth dreamed up by my imagination. Which means I’m free now, to imagine anything - worlds full of goblins and dragons and three-breasted-mer-nymphs if I want to. Why would I imagine my broken, scared and fearful soul from decades ago and feed it today?</em>

<em>Has this anything to do with Covid? Life is still happening and we are all experiencing it in our unique moment-by-moment way and I have a diary to write and an imagination to write it with - I wonder are billions of people on lockdown imagining too?</em>', 'EXCERPT: Everyday feels like Saturday to me. Which is good. Imagine a life where everyday has that Saturday morning, eat rashers for breakfast and do whatever you want to do, feeling!', '/files/uploads/2020/04/BP-Garden-Flowers.jpg', '[{"slug":"covid19diaries","title":"Covid-19 Diaries"},{"slug":"harrysdiary","title":"Harry''s Diary"}]', '[{"slug":"changingireland","title":"#ChangingIreland"},{"slug":"communityresponseirl","title":"#CommunityResponseIRL"},{"slug":"covid-19-diary","title":"Covid-19 Diary"}]', 'Changing Ireland', 249649, 0, '2020-04-24T18:18:18Z', '2020-04-24T18:18:18Z')
