INSERT INTO posts (title, slug, content, excerpt, image_url, categories, tags, author, wp_id, published, created_at, updated_at)
VALUES ('Anti-poverty programmes must take account of community trauma', 'post-254502', 'The organisation, which unfortunately has much experience and expertise in the matter, warned that failure to take community trauma into account with regard to the design of government programmes “is a terrible mistake”.

Community trauma may be a new term to many of us, and no doubt there are some who encounter it on a daily basis without realising what it is.

According to the report by Dublin City Community Co-op: “Community trauma refers to the psychological and emotional impact of a distressing event or series of events on a community or a group of people from a specific area. This type of trauma can result from: acts of violence, social injustice, forced migration or other collective experiences that disrupt the safety, stability, and wellbeing of a community.

“Trauma is a significant public health issue, negatively impacting a range of health outcomes that disproportionately impact vulnerable populations including individuals and families with low incomes, those who come from racial, ethnic or immigrant minority backgrounds, and those who are homeless.”

In an Irish context, this could relate to events from our history, such as colonisation, the Famine, or the Troubles in Northern Ireland.

“More recent events such as the Covid-19 pandemic, drug-related violence and the riots that occurred in Dublin city centre on the 23rd of November 2023 have also been seen to have a significant impact on communities,” says the report.

The collective impact of community trauma requires a “collaborative approach” between the various groups, organisations, and individuals in the affected locality.

The report states that traumatic events “can fracture the social cohesion and trust within a community”, which can lead to a decrease in social interaction, which can then result in physical, mental, and emotional symptoms.

It continued: “Community trauma can have enduring effects on individuals and communities, influencing mental health, physical health, social dynamics, and economic wellbeing for years or even generations.

“Collective trauma differs from that of individual trauma as it can live on beyond the lives of those directly impacted by the event and continue to affect future generations.”

Certain factors can make particular communities more vulnerable to the impact of traumatic events, such as: socioeconomic status, racial or ethnic background, access to resources, prior exposure to trauma, and communities exposed to long periods of crime, drugs and violence.

The report points out that the Travelling community and the Roma community “deserve special mention” as both experience some of the greatest degrees of disadvantage among minority groups in Ireland.

The report also highlights the six principles involved in providing Trauma Informed Care (TIC) to individuals and groups affected by community trauma: safety, transparency, peer support, collaboration, cultural issues, and empowerment.

Dublin City Community Co-op has several programmes currently being implemented within inner city Dublin, working with a range of minority and disadvantaged groups in the community.

The report explained: “The Coop’s Healthy Communities Project implements two programmes on a weekly basis that take a trauma informed approach, these include the weekly coffee mornings and the Capacitar Stress and Wellbeing programmes (these are programmes offering a holistic approach to healing trauma through body, mind, and spiritual practices).

“The weekly coffee mornings aim to provide a safe space for individuals to meet and have a chat with other members of the community. They have the freedom to discuss issues that may have been impacting them and to also socialise and build relationships with others in their community.

“The individuals that take part in the coffee mornings come from a range of backgrounds and experiences and may have differing levels of trauma impacting their lives, including both individual and community trauma.”

The Co-op also incorporates facilitative and reflective spaces for its staff members in order to be better able to respond to complex situations.

For example, the Co-op team participated in a facilitated session to reflect on and process the events and impact of the November 2023 Dublin city riots and to share their perspectives on what happened.

“There is no doubt it can be argued that the regular practice of ‘a brave space’ within communities is urgently required. This is necessary if we are to overcome the polarisation and division that threatens not only individual communities but the integrity of Ireland as whole and the safety and wellbeing of all its inhabitants.

The risk of abandoning an entire generation of communities to a new layer of collective trauma is becoming ever more real,” the report noted.

Dublin City Community Co-op concluded that inner city Dublin has been impacted by many traumatic events, including gang violence and a 40-year drugs crisis, which has resulted in collective trauma that is “historical as well as contemporary and ever-evolving”.

However, it found “very few studies that have captured the full effect collective community trauma has had on the members of the community living in the area”.

The organisation believes that further research “specific to the impact of community trauma in inner city Dublin would be very beneficial when incorporating trauma informed practices into programmes and government policy”.

To download the report, go to dublincitycommunitycoop.ie/', 'When Dublin City Community Coop launched its briefing document titled Understanding Community Trauma earlier this year, it remarked that the issue is “often neglected, or worse, simply ignored” with regard to policy-making.', '/files/uploads/2024/10/Generic-Garda-chopper-overhead.jpg', '[{"slug":"inclusion","title":"Inclusion"}]', '[{"slug":"community-development","title":"community development"},{"slug":"community-trauma","title":"community trauma"},{"slug":"disadvantaged-communities","title":"disadvantaged communities"},{"slug":"dublin-city-community-co-op","title":"Dublin City Community Co-Op"},{"slug":"inclusion","title":"Inclusion"}]', 'Kathy Masterson', 254502, 0, datetime('now'), datetime('now'))
