INSERT INTO posts (title, slug, content, excerpt, image_url, categories, tags, author, wp_id, published, created_at, updated_at)
VALUES ('You don''t get to hate Coolock. You don''t get to hate it unless you love it, says Kelly Earley', 'post-254505', 'In July, Kelly wrote with concern at how protests against government plans to accommodate asylum seekers locally in Coolock, Finglas
and Ballyfermot were “furthering the disdain that people on the outside have towards these communities”.

In her essay, she objected to the flawed logic that sees more police as the solution. “That’s the easy thing to do. And it’s incredibly shortsighted.”

She objects also to writing people off as “scumbags”.

“As a country, we’ve spent decades getting defensive and angry when things kick off with working class people. We rarely take a step back and apply a bit of empathy to these situations, and that’s something that is necessary. You are looking at people who have been surrounded by addiction, violence, abuse, and poverty for decades. This is not an exaggeration.”

<img src="/files/uploads/2024/10/Kelly-Earley-3.jpg" alt="" width="1200" height="1600" /> <em>- Kelly Earley</em>

Inviting outsiders to put themselves in the shoes of someone from Coolock, she writes: “Think of all of the problems you already have in your life: trying to make ends meet in a housing crisis, worrying about your job security, worrying about the health of family members, trying to save for a mortgage while inflation pushes the price of a bottle of deodorant up to €9 in Tesco.

"Life is extremely difficult for the majority of us right now. (Consider that) while living in a community where violence, drug use and crime have been completely normalised.”

She wrote hoping that people would better appreciate the level of trauma in people’s lives in Coolock and similar places and, while she has
a successful career herself today, she knows she was lucky.

Kelly speaks from experience: “I spent the majority of my life thinking that abuse, violence, crime, and poverty are normal.”

She says people living in slightly quieter places such as Swords, Artane, Raheny, Donnycarney, Clarehall and Santry know what she means.
However, in Coolock it is intensified.

“People in Coolock have been failed on every level” and Kelly feels it is “very important” to convey how badly the community has been let down.

She urges outsiders to see Coolock protesters “as human beings, who at best, have had a rough go of things". "You might not like them, or what they are doing, but please remember that these are people who have never really been given a productive outlet or framework for their frustrations.” Kelly detests racist outsiders exploiting local people''s concerns.

<img src="/files/uploads/2024/10/QR-Code-Kelly-Earleys-Coolock-article-on-Substack.png" alt="" width="450" height="450" /> <em>- Scan the QR Code above to access Kelly Earley''s article in full</em>

The following are edited extracts from Kelly''s article:

"I saw my classmates come to school beaten black and blue, with burns all over their bodies, and nothing packed in their bags for lunch. There were kids riddled with head lice, kids who could barely read by the time they got to sixth class, and kids who were already smoking
cigarettes before they had hit a double-digit birthday.

"People have always faced significant trauma in areas like Coolock, where everyone knows each other. Yet, the mental health resources are among the worst in the country. This community bears witness to regular instances of extreme physical violence, murder, and events that would leave towns in the rest of Ireland reeling. We are told: ''This is normal.''

"Resources are difficult to access everywhere – but in Coolock, the cards truly are stacked against people and everyone’s left fighting for scraps when it comes to the limited resources on offer."

"Misery comes in bucketloads, but people maintain their wit and sense of humour. Within the chaos, there is so much hope. There is joy. People have so much love for their family and friends. They look after each other to the extent that they can. People are proud of themselves, their work, their crafts, and what they bring to the table. There is truly nowhere in the world I love more than Coolock on a sunny day. There’s nothing like it."

"This isn’t just Coolock’s problem. As long as the Government continues to obliterate public services, refuse to build affordable and social housing in order to placate vulture funds and landlords, we are going to continue to see people lashing out."

"I can see why people are compelled to destroy something and express their frustrations outwardly as violence."

"If you’re finding yourself agreeing with the far right voices that are getting louder across our communities, I implore you to please ask more questions."

"Our problems existed long before the conflicts that are driving refugees to Ireland for safety and a better life. If we continue down this path, our problems will continue to exist for much longer again."

"Let’s say people are successful in this campaign of hatred and violence towards refugees. Then what? Chances are, you’re next on the list of the far right’s targets."

"Think about the rhetoric knocking around right now about migrants and refugees that has been pushed and perpetuated by the far right:
''The state hands everything to them, while the rest of us have to get up and go to work every day. Why should they get free houses while the rest of us have to work? They contribute nothing to society.'' Not too long ago, these are the exact same things that well off people in Dublin would say about those living in Coolock, or Cabra, or Blanchardstown, or Tallaght, or any other disadvantaged area you can think of."

"The tools that have been used to oppress us for decades are being handed to us by the far right, and it’s our responsibility to not use them to beat down people who are more vulnerable than us."

"If you choose to punch down, congratulations. In the eyes of the far right and our Government, who’ve historically seen us as the lowest of the low, you’re a little bit taller than you were before – but you’re standing on someone else’s neck."

"The only way we can move forward is with empathy. This applies both within Coolock, and in the context of immigration, as well as in Ireland more broadly."

"Do not let the far right dehumanise refugees and migrants any further because I promise you, there are many people outside of Coolock with their own prejudices and biases who see all of us in a similar way. To the people who are enforcing and upholding institutional violence, we’re all unvetted scroungers. We have far more in common with refugees fleeing war than we have in common with any millionaire or landlord TD," writes Kelly.', 'The headline here comes from the title of an article written by a woman from Coolock who had had enough when the media, politicians and the public were dissing her community because of protests over the pending arrival of asylum seekers.', '/files/uploads/2024/10/Kelly-Earley-4.jpg', '[{"slug":"community","title":"Community"}]', '[{"slug":"community-trauma","title":"community trauma"},{"slug":"coolock","title":"Coolock"},{"slug":"disadvantaged-communities","title":"disadvantaged communities"},{"slug":"international-protection-applicants","title":"International Protection Applicants"}]', 'Allen Meagher', 254505, 0, datetime('now'), datetime('now'))
