INSERT INTO posts (title, slug, content, excerpt, image_url, categories, tags, author, wp_id, published, created_at, updated_at)
VALUES ('Government should take same committed approach to Coolock as it does to Dublin metro', 'post-254510', 'Changing Ireland spoke with Kelly by phone following the strong and positive response to her article about Coolock and those who have let it become what it is. We asked her to tell us more than she did in the article about what needs to be done.

Kelly believes the cycle of poverty and deprivation can be broken. If the government approached Coolock like they do the metro link to Dublin Airport and planned long-term then deprivation could become a memory.

“Right now so many people can’t imagine a future for themselves past the age of 21. They have no plan and no thoughts about where they’re going to end up,” she said.

“There needs to be a proper inter-generational initiative from the government. Coolock won’t get better from five years of funding. We need to carry an entire generation out of deprivation and towards the standard of living that other parts of Dublin have. Even after that, don’t turn your back on them. Two generations of that work would go a long way.”

<img src="/files/uploads/2024/10/QR-Code-Kelly-Earleys-Coolock-article-on-Substack.png" alt="" width="450" height="450" /> Scan the QR Code above to access Kelly Earley''s article in full

“Take my friends at school who had kids when they were 18 or 19 - by the time they’re in their mid-30s, early 40s, their kids will be coming out of secondary school. Those kids should be targeted for intensive support," she says.

In Kelly’s ideal world, girls in particular would be educated on contraception, primed to go to college, and have stable financial circumstances, while boys are not attracted to easy money through drugs and crime.

Kelly also has concerns over the foster care system. While she is aware of specific housing provisions for people aged 18 who are leaving foster care, she believes the care system needs reform.

“Look at the what we have. Foster care lasts until people are 18 and then they’re pushed out into mainstream society. Most people suffer. There should be more thought put into longer-term supports,” she said.', 'Changing Ireland spoke with Kelly by phone following the strong and positive response to her article about Coolock and those who have let it become what it is. We asked her to tell us more than she did in the article about what needs to be done. Kelly believes the cycle of poverty and deprivation can be broken. If the government approached Coolock like they do the metro link to Dublin Airport and planned long-term then deprivation could become a memory. “Right now so many people can’t imagine a…', '/files/uploads/2024/10/Dublins-metrolink-connections-whenever-finished.png', '[{"slug":"community","title":"Community"}]', '[{"slug":"coolock","title":"Coolock"},{"slug":"deprivation","title":"deprivation"},{"slug":"disadvantaged-communities","title":"disadvantaged communities"},{"slug":"dublin","title":"Dublin"},{"slug":"inclusion","title":"Inclusion"},{"slug":"poverty","title":"Poverty"}]', 'Allen Meagher', 254510, 0, datetime('now'), datetime('now'))
