INSERT INTO posts (title, slug, content, excerpt, image_url, categories, tags, author, wp_id, published, created_at, updated_at)
VALUES ('Art draws young people out of themselves', 'post-254573', 'A new kind of youth club in Dundalk, Co Louth, funded by Creative Ireland and run in collaboration with the HSE, prides itself on being
led by young people for young people. It has a small group acting as ‘ambassadors’ who led in designing the project and in choosing its name.

The Blooming Minds Club is geared at young people who have been engaged with the HSE’s Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services (CAMHS).

“They chose the name Blooming Minds. We had called it Áit Eile, but they changed the name. It''s all about what they want to do or what they think their peers might like to do,” Sarah Daly, executive director of Creative Spark.

The project is only open to young people who have engaged with CAMHS and they can either self-refer or apply through their clinician. Their privacy is important.

“It is really just about supporting them. It''s a fantastic project because the funding is generous, and we''re able to do really nice projects with them that they don''t have to pay for and that they wouldn''t normally access,” she said.

The funder is Creative Ireland, which was established by the government in 2017 to promote creativity and wellbeing at individual, community and national levels.

To learn how the club works we spoke to art therapist and lead artist with the project Jenny Slater.
<h4>People First</h4>
“One of the things that I took from the funders in Creative Ireland is that it''s really about quality work over quantity and numbers. It''s really nice to have that kind of permission to design something alongside the young people with their needs at the forefront,” said Jenny.

In July, club members attended a five-day summer camp, which saw them take part in workshops with high-profile artists such as Megan
McGuigan of Seeking Judy, mural artist Omin, duo Adrian+Shane, and painter Paddy Critchley. The young people tried out embroidery, screen printing, mural design, spraypainting, pop art, digital fabrication and painting. (Digital fabrication is using computers to design and manufacture items).

Jenny said numbers are kept small so as not to overwhelm the participants. “Every day we had, on average, about eight young people attend over five days. It''s not huge numbers, but the reason we have it small is because the young people do have needs. Some will be out of school. Because of the challenges that they might have had in the past, we don''t want to be overwhelming.

She explained that “a lot” of the young people reported being isolated and not having had experiences with other people their own age in a long time.

<img src="/files/uploads/2024/10/Jenny-Slater.jpg" alt="" width="1080" height="1080" /> <em>- Jenny Slater, Blooming Minds art therapist and lead artist</em>
<h4>Novel and Inclusive</h4>
“It''s probably quite novel to have CAHMS and Creative Spark and myself collaborating. It''s also novel that the focus is about getting the voice of young people at the forefront. So it''s (inclusive of) young people who maybe aren''t necessarily asked about their opinions on things, or they''re not as included in sports or other activities. They’re a slightly marginalised group.

“I know there''s lots of art classes and things out there, but for a group of young people who''ve experienced or are experiencing mental health difficulties, it''s that extra barrier that is just so hard to get around. We''re thinking about that all the time and trying to (break down) the barriers, getting ideas from the young people themselves about ‘How could we actually encourage somebody who''s really anxious to make that first step?’.”

Jenny said the youths who serve as ambassadors have been very helpful. “They''ve come up with ideas like offering to come early to groups and do a meet and greet. So a young person could be there to welcome another young person and let them know a bit about who''s going to be in the group. Things we don''t necessarily think of.”
<h4>Confidence Building</h4>
An aim of the project is confidence building, allowing young people to try new things and explore new talents they may not have realised they had. They don’t need to be good at art. The focus is on creating a welcoming space for young people, exploring creativity and creating connections. Events they advertise through CAMHS fill up quickly. “We''re all creative, but sometimes we don''t have access to it,” said Jenny.

The club has already begun to see results. Jenny revealed: “One of the dads came to collect his young person at the end of camp last week and he said: ‘She''s like a different person. She''s up early ready to go. She''s excited to talk about it at home and she''s telling everybody what she''s been doing’. That''s really cool for us to hear that.

“Confidence and connection and self-esteem and a sense of belonging were our goals at the start. Now we''re starting to see that it seems to be having a positive effect.”

While the young people tend not to discuss their mental health when taking part in the club, Jenny says anxiety is one of the main issues affecting them, as well as challenges relating to neurodiversity. “They tend not to bring that into the group with us,” said Jenny. The focus is on strengths and positives.
<h4>Funding for two years</h4>
The Blooming Minds Club is funded under the Creative Ireland Nurture Fund for a maximum time period of 24 months. If further funding were to become available, Jenny says “certainly, we’d like it to extend”. The project entered its second year in September. “As far as we know, the funding isn''t extended or anything like that.”
<h4>Young people can be forgotten</h4>
“We''re hoping to gather research as we go along. We''ve been working with Creative Ireland, while Dundalk Institute of Technology and Dr Áine McHugh are working to capture feedback.”

Looking at the bigger picture in terms of youth services, Jenny stresses the importance of “really looking at the gaps”. Often it can be difficult for young people, particularly those experiencing mental health challenges, to find accessible services of a creative nature.

She concluded: “Sport is excellent, but what if you''re not sporty? Sometimes art courses are all for younger children. For young people with mental health difficulties, there''s that extra barrier of trying to overcome anxiety or low mood or low energy, to have the courage to step into a new environment.

“We''re trying to be innovative about how we bring those young people into places where they feel like their voice matters, where they feel like they belong, because I think it''s really easy for them to be kind of forgotten about.”

<strong>One of nine projects</strong>

Blooming Minds is one of only nine projects selected nationally to receive funding through the Creative Ireland Nurture Fund, an initiative from the Department of Tourism, Culture, Arts, Gaeltacht, Sport and Media.

The others are creative projects for young people with disabilities, young Travellers, young people from the refugee and asylum-seeking communities, residents of Oberstown Children’s Detention Campus, marginalised communities, and young people transitioning from care.
• www.creativeireland.gov.ie/', '- Funding focus in Dundalk youth project is on quality work over quantity', '/files/uploads/2024/10/Blooming-Minds-Club-scaled.jpg', '[{"slug":"education-training","title":"Education & Training"}]', '[{"slug":"adrianshane","title":"Adrian+Shane"},{"slug":"art","title":"art"},{"slug":"blooming-minds","title":"Blooming Minds"},{"slug":"child-and-adolescent-mental-heath-services-cahms","title":"Child and Adolescent Mental Heath Services CAHMS"},{"slug":"co-louth","title":"Co Louth"},{"slug":"creative-ireland","title":"Creative Ireland"},{"slug":"creative-spark","title":"Creative Spark"},{"slug":"digital-fabrication","title":"digital fabrication"},{"slug":"dundalk","title":"Dundalk"},{"slug":"dundalk-institute-of-technology","title":"Dundalk Institute of Technology"},{"slug":"hse-health-service-executive","title":"HSE"},{"slug":"mental-health","title":"mental health"},{"slug":"omin","title":"Omin"},{"slug":"paddy-critchley","title":"Paddy Critchley"},{"slug":"seeking-judy","title":"Seeking Judy"},{"slug":"wellbeing","title":"wellbeing"}]', 'Kathy Masterson', 254573, 0, datetime('now'), datetime('now'))
