INSERT INTO posts (title, slug, content, excerpt, image_url, categories, tags, author, wp_id, published, created_at, updated_at)
VALUES ('Idea for Louth Urban Food Sanctuary hatched after new species of birds landed in Dundalk', 'post-254581', 'Following further conversations on climate change and how this could affect people’s diets in the not so distant future, the Louth Urban Food Sanctuary was launched in October 2023.

Sarah Daly, executive director of Dundalk-based organisation Creative Spark, said, “It started with a conversation about climate change and what it''s doing in Louth, and the fact that birds that used to fly to Kerry now are stopping in Dundalk Bay because it''s that much warmer,” explained Sarah.

“Dundalk Bay has always been a big bird sanctuary, but different types of birds are stopping here now in the summer. That started conversations about how our landscape is changing, and (how that might change) what we eat, and what we can grow?”

Farmer Tony McGuinness, who runs a farm with his brother Pat in Ardee, Co Louth, was on board from the beginning. The brothers are diversifying their produce, and are currently growing test crops of peas, broad beans, sunflower, red and white millet, linseed and quinoa.

Louth Urban Food Sanctuary also has five community gardens around the county, and a group of 20 local growers who grow food on a small scale in their gardens or allotments. The locals are testing foods such as ginger, turmeric, chilies, pak choy, sweet potatoes and herbs that generally need hotter weather to grow.

<img src="/files/uploads/2024/10/LUFS-Team-2.jpg" alt="" width="1200" height="800" /> <em>- Louth Urban Food Sanctuary is a "citizen science" project that encourages people to grow their own food and eat local produce.</em>

Sarah added: “People are testing them in their gardens, or on their windowsills, or greenhouses if they have access to one. And we''re going to work with DKIT on recipes for some of the foods that we''ve grown. They have a culinary school in the college.”

Louth Urban Food Sanctuary hosts regular workshops and guest speakers. “We''ve had a couple of hundred people involved so far. The first event had over 60 people here and they learned how to pot garlic, because we can grow garlic in Ireland and we should be growing it, but we import tons of it from Spain and China.” (They even have a garlic tutorial video on their website - https://louthurbanfoodsanctuary.com/)

Of the overall project, Sarah said: “It''s a kind of citizen science. What''s happening in the climate and what we can do to be more sustainable ourselves and also futureproof our own food supplies, and try and eat local and grow local.”

The Louth Urban Food Sanctuary received €48,000 in funding through the government''s Creative Climate Action Fund II. Louth County Council leads the project and it partnered with Creative Spark. National body Creative Ireland also provides support and the project celebrates its first birthday this October.

<img src="/files/uploads/2024/10/Creative-Spark-Downtown-Hub-scaled.jpg" alt="" width="2560" height="1707" /> <em>- The Creative Spark Downtown Hub in Dundalk, Co Louth</em>
<h4>What is Creative Spark?</h4>
Established in Dundalk, Co Louth, in 2012, Creative Spark is a not-for-profit social enterprise that aims to develop the creative industries sector in the north-east, and to support new and established innovative enterprises.

It originally set out to provide training and workspace facilities for creative enterprises in Louth and beyond, but now does all that and more.
Sarah Daly, executive director of Creative Spark explains: “We have about 54 small businesses based here between own-door office spaces and shared co-working spaces. Then we have our fine art print studios. We have 33 members and they can come in and access our facilities here.

“Last year we added FabLab,” she continued. “Its aim is to work with small businesses and enable people to use digital fabrication in their businesses and we have a lot of creative people using that now. We''re trying to encourage more small engineering and manufacturing businesses to come in and use it to speed up their processes and learn more about digital fabrication.”

Creative Spark also runs a runs a weekly children’s club and runs creative classes all year round, covering everything from painting to ceramics, dressmaking – including for children.

Sarah added: “We try and make those affordable, and we offer three places on all the courses at a 50 per cent discount to anybody on benefits, so we''re trying to give something back to the community. We don''t really make any money out of those, we just cover the costs of these courses.”', 'The idea for the Louth Urban Food Sanctuary was hatched after locals in Dundalk noticed new species of birds arriving in Dundalk Bay in summertime.', '/files/uploads/2024/10/LUFS-Launch-10.jpg', '[{"slug":"climate-justice","title":"Climate Justice"}]', '[{"slug":"art","title":"art"},{"slug":"climate-action","title":"climate action"},{"slug":"climate-change","title":"climate change"},{"slug":"co-louth","title":"Co Louth"},{"slug":"creative-climate-action-fund","title":"Creative Climate Action Fund"},{"slug":"creative-enterprise","title":"creative enterprise"},{"slug":"creative-ireland","title":"Creative Ireland"},{"slug":"creative-spark","title":"Creative Spark"},{"slug":"creative-spark-downtown-hub","title":"Creative Spark Downtown Hub"},{"slug":"digital-fabrication","title":"digital fabrication"},{"slug":"dundalk","title":"Dundalk"},{"slug":"fablab","title":"FabLab"},{"slug":"louth-urban-food-sanctuary","title":"Louth Urban Food Sanctuary"},{"slug":"small-business","title":"small business"}]', 'Kathy Masterson', 254581, 0, datetime('now'), datetime('now'))
