VALUES ('Older people being made homeless "is very solvable, very quickly"', 'older-people-being-made-homeless-is-very-solvable-very-quickly', '<span>In the last five years there has been a 104% increase in the number of people aged over 65 experiencing homelessness, rising from 122 in 2020 to 249 in June of this year.</span>

<span>While the picture is darkening, she feels it is still at a level where a lot can be done and a severe crisis can be prevented. If the will is there.</span>

<span>“The last couple of months the homeless figures have kept increasing. I think people can feel hopeless and despondent, we’re constantly putting forward solutions and asking for things, and we are maybe heard but not listened to. But we are really trying to put a spotlight on the number of over 65s in emergency accommodation, because it’s one of the fastest growing cohorts. There are about 250 people in that cohort across the country, so it is a very small, manageable number right now,” said Ber.</span>

<span>The likes of Simon tend to become aware of emerging problems first, she says, and their perspective should inform decision making. </span>

<span>She also says that if the views of Simon are taken on board, an emerging issue can be headed off.</span>

<span>“Frontline services tend to see these trends before they’re reflected at a policy level. We’ve seen it previously with family homelessness, with young people-18-24 year olds. That’s why we’re trying to raise this now, particularly while there are decisions being made about pensions and things. It is manageable. It is very solvable, very quickly, and that’s what we’re asking Government to do, and it’s the reason we’re putting it in the spotlight.”</span>

<span>The numbers of people affected is not astronomical, and she says most of them don’t have some of the complex issues that often haunt younger homeless people. </span>

<span>“Instead of thinking about over 16,000 people in emergency accommodation we’re saying here is a really small cohort of people, this is what you need to be looking at now. If people are over 65 entering homelessness for the first time they may have more physical health or accessibility needs, but they are less likely to have any of the additional more complex needs that you might have if you are experiencing homelessness long term. If you’re someone who was maybe previously donating to and volunteering with homelessness charities, and because of the unaffordability or rents you are in a situation where you need to access services; you maybe don’t have those additional mental health needs. It is literally about affordable accommodation.”</span>

<span>On the broader question of homelessness, she says there are actions that can be taken that will work, and that the over 65s is a good place to make progress.</span>

<span>“During Covid, that was the first time we saw a dip in the numbers of people experiencing homelessness, so we know that the flow of people into homelessness can be stopped. But for whatever reason there is a reluctance on the Government’s side to bring back in those measures that are needed. </span>

<span>“We’re saying that this is such a small number of people over 65, but it’s going to become a bigger issue because we know that less people will own their houses or have mortgages paid off. They’re looking at the My Future Fund and the autoenrolment for pensions. If they’re forward planning for that they need to take housing security into account as well.”</span>

<span>In general, she feels that progress can be made if there is a proactive political approach. </span>

<span>“People need to see real action being taken. There is kind of an acceptance that the numbers are going up every month. We’ve heard the Taoiseach say that it’s people refusing social housing allocations and things like that, but those aren’t the real reasons. It’s the unaffordability of the private rental sector and the inaccessibility of the private rental sector. There’s no use in just pushing back with excuses, we need real action to happen.”</span>

<span>Simon Week 2025,  a week-long campaign which saw Simon Communities of Ireland run education and awareness events in a number of locations across the country and undertake local and national lobbying, took place in late September.</span>

<span>While she felt it was positive, the real impact of it will be revealed in the budget. “There was good engagement from elected representatives last week, but the proof will be in the pudding in Budget 2026, whether or not they will focus on prevention. We’ve really tried to start talking about the prevention piece,” she said.
</span>

&nbsp;', 'While there is an increasing number of older people experiencing homelessness, there is also capacity for the State to make a lot of progress on the issue fairly quickly, if the nettle is grasped. That’s the view of Ber Grogan, Executive Director of Simon Communities, which held a conference in Galway on the issue in Galway on September 26.', '/files/uploads/2025/09/Simon-Conference-2025-17-scaled-e1761708958517.jpg', '[{"slug":"equality-rights","title":"Equality & Rights"},{"slug":"policy-strategy","title":"Policy & Strategy"}]', '[{"slug":"homeless","title":"homeless"},{"slug":"over-65s","title":"over 65s"},{"slug":"simon","title":"Simon"}]', 'Owen Ryan', 255450, 0, datetime('now'), datetime('now'))
