VALUES ('Loneliness "widespread and persistent" among older men', 'post-255495', '<span>It found that while community based initiatives like Men’s Sheds and hobbies do provide support, many are still isolated emotionally, even when they maintain contact with family or live in shared environments.</span>

<span>As bad as that might sound, in Romania loneliness rates hit 60% in some urban areas. </span>

<b>Help and support</b>

<span>The report stated that elderly people in Ireland often have a good deal of local support.</span>

<span> “In 2019, 84% of older people aged 75 years and over shared that they find it easy or very easy to get help from neighbours and 75% of them have at least three people they could count on if they had a serious problem. According to Eurostat data (2024), 19.6% of men over 65 years old in Ireland engaged in formal or informal voluntary activities or active citizenship. The figures for Ireland are above the EU average (11.8%).”</span>

<span>It found that for older people in Ireland, not being able to drive can be isolating. “Older people’s participation in community life can be significantly reduced following a loss of the ability to drive, inability to afford a car, or the death of a spouse who was the household’s driver. It was found that older drivers are less lonely than non-drivers. However, non-driving men are more likely to report intimate social relationships, as their reliance on others for lifts may provide opportunities for social interactions with others.”</span>

<span>Older people in Ireland are quite likely to live in substandard accommodation, while they also face significant financial issues.</span>

<span>“At least 48% of older adults in Ireland live in housing that does not meet the UN adequacy standard. The most significant issue is that half of their homes have a Building Energy Rating of E, F, or G. Additionally, an estimated 20% of older households cannot afford essential maintenance or repairs. At least 10% lack the financial means to adapt their homes for improved accessibility for individuals with disabilities or impairments. Furthermore, 13.3% of people over 65 years old live in homes with structural issues such as leaking roofs, damp walls, damaged floors, or decaying window frames.</span>

<span>“The median equivalised nominal disposable income for those aged 65 years was €25,986 in 2023, an increase from €22,027 in 2020 (CSO, 2024). While many older men may own homes, they may still face financial challenges, including limited disposable income, making it difficult to engage in social opportunities.”</span>

<b>Work and age</b>

<span>The research stated that older people face a range of barriers to continuing in employment. </span>

<span>“Many individuals in their 60s leave the workforce, often due to long-term illness, disability, or the decision to retire. However, this trend is also influenced by a lack of suitable employment opportunities. Additionally, many employment contracts still enforce mandatory retirement at the age of 65, a practice that is still legally permissible.”</span>

<span>It found that depictions of older people in the Irish media tend to be negative. </span>

<span>“Older people in Irish newspapers are often portrayed as dependent and separate from mainstream society. They are categorised usually into five distinct identity types: ''victims,'' ''frail, infirm and vulnerable,'' ''radicalized citizens,'' ''deserving old,'' and ''undeserving old.'' Notably, the portrayal of older people as healthy and autonomous is largely absent.”</span>

<span>It warned that the closure of pubs could be particularly isolating for older people. </span>

<span>“Pub closures are increasingly common, especially in rural areas, where their role is particularly important due to the common lack of other activities and social outlets. In fact, in many rural areas, pubs are the only spaces allowing for this kind of social engagement, community-building, and communal activities.</span>

<span>“With the progressive, generational decline of pub culture in Ireland, as well as differences in ageing across rural and urban areas, this socialising function of pubs is particularly relevant to older people. Accordingly, the closure of pubs particularly threatens the social functioning and connectedness of older people.”</span>

<span>A small number of older men were interviewed as part of the research and some of them spoke of their own experience of loneliness.</span>

<span>“I''m lonely. Everything''s very good, until you go to your own house and close your door. And there’s two things that have been a comfort to me when I''m at home: football, and television. But imagine, 100 years ago, when someone had nothing inside the door, near the fire, and that was it. We have a lot.” said Frank, a 74-year-old from Longford.</span>

<span>John, a 73 year old, said he would like to meet a partner, but was concerned about losing property. “</span>

<span>“There''s a lot of lonely men in Ireland anyway. I''ve been talking to men, and they''re lonely. And men in Ireland are afraid to meet a partner, because they''re afraid that they''ll meet a woman and she''ll live with them for a while, then take the land and the house off the man, and he''s out. I’d just like to meet somebody, but I''m afraid I’ll end up losing another house.”  </span>

<span>The research found that across all the countries surveyed, loneliness was a significant problem among older people, but it claimed there are things that can be done to alleviate it.</span>

<span>“Across all three countries, findings underline the importance of accessible community structures, emotional engagement, and maintaining interests as key to addressing loneliness—mere physical presence or occasional contact is not enough without deeper social and emotional ties.”</span>

<b>Improvements to be made</b>

<span>It listed six steps that should be taken to improve the level of loneliness among the elderly.</span>

<span>These are improving public perceptions of ageing and mental health; raising awareness of available services and supports; enhancing and budgeting social structures for older men; improving digital connectivity; enhancing access and mobility and finally ensuring economic security. </span>

<span>Bernie Curran, General Manager of Friends of the Elderly, commented on the findings: “This </span><span>research confirms what we see every day in our work: loneliness is a silent epidemic that has a devastating impact on the health and wellbeing of all older people but especially older men. While family support is important, it is often not enough. This report highlights the power of community and connection, as one participant powerfully stated, finding others in a similar position is ‘a powerful medicine’. We are dedicated to providing the friendship and support that helps alleviate this isolation, and this research will be instrumental in shaping policies and services that ensure no one is left feeling alone.”</span>', 'New research on loneliness among older men in Ireland, Poland and Romania, shows that it is “a widespread and persistent issue”. The research was commissioned by Friends of the Elderly, based in Ireland. It found found that, “In Ireland, approximately one-third of older people report feeling lonely, particularly those who are single or managing chronic conditions.', '/files/uploads/2025/10/The_most_terrible_poverty_is_loneliness_and_the_feeling_of_being_unloved.jpg', '[{"slug":"health","title":"Health"},{"slug":"inclusion","title":"Inclusion"},{"slug":"people","title":"People"}]', '[{"slug":"elderly","title":"elderly"},{"slug":"friends-of-the-elderly","title":"Friends of the Elderly"},{"slug":"research","title":"research"}]', 'Owen Ryan', 255495, 0, datetime('now'), datetime('now'))
