INSERT INTO posts (title, slug, content, excerpt, image_url, categories, tags, author, wp_id, published, created_at, updated_at)
VALUES ('Budget broadly welcomed, but "lacklustre" in parts says CRA', 'post-255502', '<span>The Children’s Rights Alliance was positive about a number of aspects of the budget, but did have a few criticisms also.</span>

<span>“There are key targeted supports announced today that sow the seeds for real change on the rising child poverty levels. However, the lacklustre action in some areas leaves the Government with a long road ahead if they are to realise their own child poverty target,” said Tanya Ward, Chief Executive of the Children’s Rights Alliance.</span>

<span>She praised the decision to raise the amount going to people relying on social welfare to support their children. </span>

<span>“We heard promise after promise, month after month, that Budget 2026 would include targeted supports to address child poverty and the Government has taken decisive action to deliver on these. Increases to the Child Support Payment – an income support specifically for children living in families on the lowest incomes, reliant on social welfare supports to get through the week – of €8 for children under 12 and €16 over 12 are significant.</span>

<span>“Not only have increases to this vital support been maintained in this budget, so too has the higher rate for older children. It shows a clear understanding from Government that to break the cycle of poverty, income supports need to adequately address the grinding costs of poverty that are a weight around the neck of too many children and families.”</span>

<span>Ms Ward also welcomed the increase in the weekly threshold of the Working Family Payment by €60 and a guarantee that families receiving it will be eligible for the fuel allowance.</span>

<span>She also welcomed a commitment that further measures on social welfare will come in future budgets.</span>

<span>“It is very positive that the social welfare package announced today comes with the commitment of a ‘multi-year’ programme. These supports need to be increased over the course of successive budgets.”</span>

<span>She also feels that the Government has taken a very positive step towards addressing disadvantage in education.</span>

<span>“The DEIS Plus programme is an example of the unapologetic and aspiring action we need to see on child poverty. Today, the political drive to deliver this programme in the lifetime of this Government is met with funding for its first year. For schools operating in areas experiencing the highest levels of deprivation, who stretch themselves to extraordinary lengths to support their students, this new programme and funding to support a new DEIS plan will make a real difference.” </span>

<span>However she was less positive about the measures included in relation to early years education</span>

<span>“Early childhood education and care is the single most effective leveller for children. Budget 2026 cannot be heralded as a child poverty budget without ramped up investment in this area. From what we have seen, the level of increased investment leaves us with more questions than certainty. Equal Start has the potential to copper-fasten the wraparound supports children and families experiencing poverty and while the continued rollout is positive, significant investment is required to ensure services have the capacity to meet the need they see.”</span>

<span>On Child Protection and welfare, she said more needs to be done. </span>

<span>“Further clarity is needed on the investment directed to our child protection and welfare system to determine how serious the Government are in supporting and protecting the safety and wellbeing of the children and young people who have no choice but to rely on the state to do so. When we know that there has been an avalanche of referrals in recent years, failing to significantly increase its capacity and funding beyond existing levels of service to meet this demand is unacceptable.” </span>

<span>Summing up, Ms Ward added, “Budget 2026 is the first of this new Government term but there is no reason why it cannot be a bold statement for what the next four to five years will be for children, young people and families. It is positive to see the blend of universal and targeted social welfare supports that are needed to effectively break the cycle of intergenerational poverty. </span>

<span>“There are clearly efforts made to strengthen social welfare supports for those most in need and ensure economic growth is in line with a just transition. We see ambitious strides once again in education to address educational disadvantage to ensure children are supported to reach their full potential. However, in other areas, it’s unclear that child poverty was a central consideration. Children deserve more than an ‘either or’ compromise in budget negotiations. Until we see cross-departmental action to address the systemic inequalities baked into our systems, the Government will be left chasing to bridge a widening gap, and children and families will be the ones losing out,” she said.
</span>

&nbsp;', 'The Children’s Rights Alliance was positive about a number of aspects of the budget, but did have a few criticisms also. “There are key targeted supports announced today that sow the seeds for real change on the rising child poverty levels. However, the lacklustre action in some areas leaves the Government with a long road ahead if they are to realise their own child poverty target,” said Tanya Ward, Chief Executive of the Children’s Rights Alliance. She praised the decision to raise the amount…', '/files/uploads/2024/10/End-Child-Poverty-Week-2024-CRA-CEO-Tanya-Ward-2.jpg', '[{"slug":"education-training","title":"Education & Training"}]', '[{"slug":"budget","title":"budget"},{"slug":"childrens-rights-alliance","title":"Children''s Rights Alliance"},{"slug":"tanya-ward","title":"Tanya Ward"}]', 'Owen Ryan', 255502, 0, '2025-10-29T00:40:40Z', '2025-10-29T00:40:40Z')
