VALUES ('‘More and more families are using foodbanks’', 'post-255642', '&nbsp;

<span>The Food Equality Initiative is a key component of Donegal County Council’s €7.6 million PEACEPLUS Local Community Action Plan, managed by the Special EU Programmes Body (SEUPB). </span>

<span>Running over 24 months, FEI will deliver 25 “Healthy, Balanced, Affordable Cooking for All” 6-week courses for over 200 participants across Donegal. The programme will bring together community groups, public bodies, retailers, and Donegal’s 21 food banks to promote access to nutritious food, social connection, and inclusion. A cross-border partnership with Acorn Farm, Derry, will strengthen cooperation across Donegal, Derry &amp; Tyrone.</span>

<b>Foodbanks</b>

<span>Mary Coyle of Ionad Naomh Pádraig in Gweedore runs a local food bank and said she welcomed the initiative.</span>

<span>Ms Coyle said that food poverty in Donegal is a huge issue, with little realisation of just how widespread it is. </span>

<span>“We are working on a strategic plan and it’s a piece of work that is being compiled by all the food banks in Donegal, there are 21 of us. Is it needed? Absolutely. Does our voice need to be heard at Government level? Absolutely. I don’t think they realise the amount of families that are being supported in Donegal by foodbanks. It used to be families on low incomes or that were receiving social welfare payments. Now we find that it is the working class families that are struggling, because it is impossible to pay for everything and still put food on the table.”</span>

<span>She has seen the issue worse and worsen, and she knows the coming weeks are going to see a big spike in demand at the county’s foodbanks. </span>

<span>“It has grown and grown. I know when I started doing this job 20 years ago, there were no foodbanks. It started off small but it has spiralled. More and more families are using foodbanks. We’re coming into Christmas which is a very difficult time, families will struggle more and more because they are trying to put money aside for other things. It’s definitely a challenging time for families.”</span>

<span>People don’t advertise that they need to visit foodbanks, and for that reason their importance can perhaps be underestimated.</span>

<span>Mary and her colleagues are lobbying for their importance to be recognised and for some support to be provided. </span>

<span>“There are 21 groups that are part of the food response network. Seven of the groups journeyed to Dublin just before the budget to see if we could entice FoodCloud to have a hub in Donegal. They have a hub elsewhere in the country but none in the northwest. Our main objective was to visit FoodCloud and see if we could entice them to send some food to Donegal. It’s very hard for people to believe (how many people rely on foodbanks), but it’s maybe a testimony to the foodbanks that they give a very confidential service and we don’t highlight it on a daily basis. But we are trying to get a bit of a change at Government levels, because foodbanks do need to be supported.”</span>

<b>Long term solutions</b>

<span>Not only do foodbanks need to be supported, but the underlying issues need to be addressed, so sticking plasters aren’t constantly being applied.</span>

<span>“What is it that families are struggling with? There’s a need to find out what the root problem is and to tackle the root problem. We can’t just keep giving families food, we need to get to the root of the problem and help families to get out of this situation that they’re in.”</span>

<span>Inflation has really soared in recent years, and she says people now run out of money midweek, with not a cent left to pay for necessities at certain times.</span>

<span>“The cost of living is too high. Rents and mortgages are sky high. Families paying for childcare, families with children going to college, these things impact on the income of a household. Some families would say that by the time they pay all of their bills they might get to Tuesday or Wednesday, the next wages day is Friday and you have nothing left.”</span>

<span>She feels it is very important to keep drawing attention to the plight of those who are struggling to feed their families, and that all programmes that help are welcome. </span>

<span>“The key thing is that we raise awareness so that people realise there are a lot of people in our community that are struggling and that need extra help. No child should go hungry.</span>

<span>“It is very sad. It should not be happening. We are taking one step forward and two back.”</span>

<b>Arranmore view</b>

<span>John McCafferty, Manager, Comharchumann Forbartha and Fostaíochta Árainn Mhór, said that on Arranmore Island there has been some improvement around food poverty, but he is aware that in many other parts of Donegal there is a big problem. </span>

<span>“We have had issues, at the moment we are doing okay. But there are groups on the mainland that are very active in trying to deal with it. Absolutely it is an issue.”</span>

<span>He is glad to see a new initiative to help and feels that in general the State needs to wake up to the problem. </span>

<span>“Anything is better than the way it is now. At the moment there is nothing there. There isn’t a State body doing the work that these local groups are doing. First of all the issue shouldn’t be there, but it is, and it needs to be dealt with. The aid should be coming from the State, not local charities or community organisations trying to bridge a gap.”</span>

&nbsp;', 'With food poverty a real problem in the county, Donegal Local Development Company (DLDC) has officially launched the Food Equality Initiative (FEI) — a programme tackling food inequality and promoting sustainable living, supported by €200,000 in PEACEPLUS funding.', '/files/uploads/2025/10/LK-Foodbank_11-1.jpg', '[{"slug":"empowerment","title":"Empowerment"}]', '[{"slug":"donegal","title":"Donegal"},{"slug":"food-equality-initiative","title":"Food Equality Initiative"},{"slug":"food-poverty","title":"food poverty"},{"slug":"mary-coyle","title":"Mary Coyle"}]', 'Owen Ryan', 255642, 0, datetime('now'), datetime('now'))
