VALUES ('"This is about language protection"', 'post-255647', '&nbsp;

<span>That’s the view of Adhna Ní Bhraonáin, founder of community group Bánú, which is looking to protect the language and make it possible for native speakers to live in the area. </span>

<span>She says the language, spoken in Connemara for centuries, will not endure much longer, if it is not possible for Irish speakers to live in their home place. </span>

<span>“It absolutely won’t, no. We’re being outbid. There are very old, two bedroom bungalows going for half a million. How is a local person going to keep up with that when we’re on normal salaries? You just won’t get a mortgage for it. There’s nothing stopping people from America, from Germany, from Dublin, coming in to buy those properties.”</span>

<span>She is aware of numerous cases where people from the Connemara Gaeltacht sought to build lives there, but had to concede defeat, because there was no way they could get housing security. </span>

<span>“This is about language protection. There is absolutely a housing crisis in the whole country, has been for a long time, but the impact it is having on the language is just brutal. Schools are closing because young families can’t locate here. I''ve had people reaching out to me who say they moved home from Australia with their kids. They tried living here for two or three years, moved around from place to place, and eventually gave up and moved back to Australia. These are cainteoirí gaeilge, from the place, who tried to move home, really wanted to be at home near family. But the quality of life they have, when they are pushed around from rental to rental, is just terrible.”</span>

<b>No housing available</b>

<span>Adhna spent years abroad herself, and when she wanted to come home was hit by the brutal reality of the housing crisis, which led to her founding Bánú. “I started it back in 2023 when I came home after five years of living abroad. I had money saved, wanted to buy a house or build a house or something. I just really struggled with the prices here, I couldn’t believe it, and there were really no supports for first time buyers. I was trying to find a rental then so that I could live in my community. </span>

<span>“I couldn’t find anywhere to rent, so the only place for me to go was into Galway City. It was stupid, I was a physio, with the Irish language, had a job in the Gaeltacht, and didn’t have anywhere to live in the Gaeltacht, except for moving back in with my parents. I did move back in with my parents and I’ve been with them since really.”</span>

<span>She says there are lots of Connemara people in Australia, who all expect to come back in the coming years, but it is actually very hard to return. </span>

<span>“Most of the people I went to school with are still in Australia, and I know they’re all going to want to come home. I just can’t see how they’re ever going to find somewhere to live.”</span>

<b>Planning issues</b>

<span>While there is sometimes a tendency to believe that big problems cannot be solved, she feels the causes are easily diagnosed and they can be dealt with.</span>

<span>“One of the major issues is that planning permission is being refused left, right and centre in the Gaeltacht, because it’s a special area of conservation. New guidelines have been promised since 2021, but there still hasn’t even been a draft. The latest promise is they’ll be out before Christmas, we’ll see about that.</span>

<span>“The other major problem is that a lot of properties in the Gaeltacht are being bought as investments or second homes. There’s not a lot we can do to stop that, but we want to see long term rentals incentivised. Nearly everyone in Connemara has a chalet, but they’re all going up on AirBnB and we want people to be encouraged to have them as long term rentals.”</span>

<b>Agencies</b>

<span>State agencies that exist to support Gaeltacht areas need to be repurposed slightly, to help tackle the major issue facing the region today.</span>

<span>“Roinn na Gaeltachta used to give people grants who were building their first home, to buy some raw materials. They are still allowed to do that under legislation, but in the economic crash after 2008 they didn’t have the money. We’re trying to get them to bring that back, but they say they don’t have the budget.</span>

<span>“Údarás na Gaeltachta have a lot of land in the Gaeltacht area. They were initially set up to provide jobs in the Gaeltacht, and they put up factories and that kind of thing. Now there are loads of jobs in the Gaeltacht but people are commuting out from Galway city because there is no housing. We want them to change their role a little bit, to use the land that they have, maybe sell it at a reasonable price with services on it like sewage and water.”</span>

<span>The Ready to Build Scheme, under which local authorities can sell service sites for up to €30,000 below market value, should also be adapted to help Connemara people, she adds. </span>

<span>“It’d be great if they could do that with a coinníoll teanga, a language requirement.”</span>

<span>While living Down Under she saw what a society that prioritises young people who want homes looks like. </span>

<span>“There were so many supports for first time buyers, you only needed a 5% deposit and they really support people to get their first home. There’s none of that here. You need help from your parents to buy a home here.”</span>

<span>The name of the organisation she founded fits almost perfectly, given the problem it is trying to address and the vision it has for the area.</span>

<span>“Bánú means depopulation, desolate, kind of abandoning of a thing. It can also mean something has robbed me of my money, and the Gaeltacht is kind of being robbed of its people and the language. But there’s also a positive turn on the word, bánú an lae means dawn of the day, and I liked that it had a positive meaning as well.”</span>

<span>She is keen to point out that Bánu is not about keeping people out, it is about giving communities and a language a chance to endure. </span>

<span>“The main goal of the whole thing is to protect the language by providing more housing. A crucial point is that we are not trying to push people out of the area. It’s about houses left empty all year round, schools and shops closing, communities suffering, and the language obviously.”</span>

&nbsp;', 'If most people were asked to name a real hotbed of the Irish language they would probably say Connemara, but it is at real risk of being wiped out there, unless there is a substantial change to housing policy.', '/files/uploads/2025/10/Adhna-Ni-Bhraonain.jpg', '[{"slug":"community","title":"Community"}]', '[{"slug":"adhna-ni-bhraonain","title":"Adhna Ní Bhraonáin"},{"slug":"banu","title":"Bánú"},{"slug":"connemara","title":"Connemara"},{"slug":"galway","title":"Galway"},{"slug":"housing","title":"housing"}]', 'Owen Ryan', 255647, 0, datetime('now'), datetime('now'))
