VALUES ('Need for successor to Retrofit Ready Programme identified', 'post-255679', '&nbsp;

<span>The project was run by a consortium which included the Irish Local Development Network, </span><span>Sligo Leader, Leitrim Local Development, County Cavan Development and Monaghan Integrated Development), PlanEnergy, and ATU Sligo. </span>

<span>Monaghan Integrated Development CEO Kieran Cawley felt it was only starting to show its potential at its conclusion.</span>

<span>“What I would say is there was a huge amount of work done in the background that had started to build a lot of momentum. It is only now that a lot of the work that was put into place is starting to come through. </span>

<span>“We are trying to look at ways to build on the work that has been done. These things take time; a period of time is needed for education, for funding and things like that. We’ve put out a call to get BER ratings done on community centres and other community buildings, to try and create more awareness and to try to move things to the next stage, actually getting retrofit work done.”</span>

<span>He said that shortage of labour had been a limiting factor. “One of the other issues is the availability of tradespeople to do the work. It’s a limiting factor. There’s a joke about a GAA club having a draw and the first prize being the phone number of an electrician.”</span>

<strong>Need to continue</strong>

<span>An updated version of the programme is required and he is hopeful that there will be one.</span>

<span>“There is definitely a push to look at another programme. The likelihood is that there will be something, but we haven’t been told what that would look like. At a local level we are looking to see what we can do, either ourselves or in partnership with some of the other counties that were on the Retrofit Programme originally.”</span>

<span>Retrofit champions were appointed through each local development company and he said it was a valuable role. “In an ideal world they would be engaged again in another format, to push the work that they have done forwards.”</span>

<span>The fact the programme was run through local development companies, rather than profit driven private providers, meant that the public had faith in it. </span>

<span>“There were very good numbers, a lot of engagement with communities. One of the big things was that the Local Development Companies and the champions were trusted and seen as honest brokers. There was no pressure put on people to make decisions or to buy or anything like that. It was a great way of getting information out, being able to signpost people to grants, give them the information that was needed and they could plan for when they would be able to do something.”</span>

<span>He said the need for a new programme has been brought to the highest level of government, with the benefits from it having been outlined. </span>

<span>Fuel poverty is a real problem across the region, he says. “There is a huge spike in the cost of electricity and fuel. That’s eating into the income of people for whom income has been quite fixed. It’s a choice of whether we eat or stay warm in a lot of cases. I’m involved in a number of different charities and we end up buying oil for people or fuel for stoves or whatever it is, just to keep people warm. It’s a huge issue.”</span>

<span>Monaghan Integrated Development do look to help people to reduce their costs with basic steps, less extensive than retrofitting. “We do a lot of education with people in terms of their own energy awareness, how they can save money with fuel, turning down thermostats and things. Simple things to try and improve awareness and to reduce the cost of heating.”</span>

<strong>Argument to continue</strong>

<span>Paul Ginnell of the European Anti Poverty Network said that in general terms supporting people around retrofitting makes an awful lot of sense.</span>

<span> “If you look at grants and so on, it’s said that people get value back in the long term from being able to install solar panels or retrofit their houses or make some adaptation. That’s fine, but if you don’t have the money to start with, to be able to invest, then you will continue to have higher energy costs. If the Government wants to achieve its climate targets, and it badly needs to and is well behind; then that’s one of the means of doing it.”</span>

<span>The removal of energy credits is making things difficult for those on lower incomes in particular, and retrofitting is an important means of combating fuel poverty, he adds.  </span>

<span>“I wouldn’t particularly agree with energy credits being universal to start with, but they’ve been removed for all households, and that’s not going to help families either. I know they have expanded the fuel allowance a bit, but really retrofitting is a key way for households to keep their costs down in the longer term. And if households can’t afford to do it, then lower income households will lose out.”</span>', 'The ending of the Retrofit Ready programme, which saw support to retrofit homes and other buildings in Cavan, Monaghan, Sligo and Leitrim had achieved a lot, and its potential was only really being seen around the time it closed.', '/files/uploads/2025/11/Killian-Cawley.jpeg', '[{"slug":"development-work","title":"Development Work"}]', '[]', 'Owen Ryan', 255679, 0, datetime('now'), datetime('now'))
