VALUES ('“I spent the next five years fighting the prejudice”', 'post-255689', '&nbsp;

<span>At the recent ‘In Our Words’ conference in Athlone, Ms Spollen said that events in her early life have given her an understanding of the impact of a lack of supports on people with particular needs.</span>

<span>“</span><span>Connections Arts Centre was developed from my own lived experience. As a non-diagnosed dyslexic with ADHD I really struggled in school, particularly secondary school. I still remember the day my Dad dropped me to an entrance exam for secondary school, which I failed miserably. I spent the next five years fighting the prejudice from that one exam. </span>

<span>“But I look back and I consider myself to be one of the very lucky people compared to some of my friends, because I was creative. I wanted to study art and when I went to art college I discovered a whole new way of learning through creativity and I really thrived and this is the experience I want to share with other people,” she said. </span>

<strong>Gaps</strong>

<span>She feels that things are improving for people with disabilities, but the reality is there are still large gaps. </span>

<span>“Twenty per cent of people in Ireland have a disability and since the State has implemented the New Directions policy, the landscape for people in Ireland with disabilities has changed dramatically. People are transitioning from large segregated campuses into community and it’s a fantastic initiative, bringing people with disabilities back into our communities. But like any initiative it comes with new problems and every day adults with disabilities struggle to access community, arts and education programmes, due to a lack of facilities designed to meet their needs.”</span>

<span>On what Connections Arts Centre does, she said, “We work under three value principles, creativity, community and change. We’re basically trying to harness the power of creativity to elevate individuals within the disabled community and instigate societal change.”</span>

<span>She said that its Connecting Art Programme &amp; Collective is one of its key activities, and has been hugely successful.</span>

<span>“The programme started in 2022 with a call out for artists with disabilities who would like to participate in a national exhibition in the Royal College of Surgeons. As opposed to just taking the art work and exhibiting, we provide a ten week online programme where the artists come together, share their skillsets, learn new skills like how to price their artwork, framing their artwork, also linking them with mentors through the programme. </span>

<span>“With the first programme we had this amazing exhibition and at the end of it we were told this can’t end, the artists said ‘we need this to keep going’.  So we developed the collective and that’s been going for the past three years. We have 30 + artists in the collective from all over Ireland and we continue to support them with opportunities to exhibit, to sell their work, to access paid opportunities.”</span>

<span>On the benefits to individuals, she gave the example of an artist named Dave Singleton, who had been extremely shy, but who has gone on to have a solo exhibition. </span>

<strong>TY</strong>

<span>She also spoke about the organisation’s Creative TY Connections programme,which sees people with disabilities partnered with young people.  “This is an innovative community education programme. It is designed to empower adults with disabilities by bridging a gap in community education.”</span>

<span>Ms Spollen spoke about one man who had been very reluctant to come to this programme on its first day, but had absolutely thrived thereafter. </span>

<span>“He has gone on to engage so much more in his community. He’s participating in group tennis lessons, he has joined baking classes. He is really thriving and that is the impact on the ground.”</span>

<span>She is looking to partner with other organisations to expand the programme.</span>

<span>“We want to make Creative TY Connections a national community education programme. We have everything in place to do that. </span>

<span>“We are looking for like minded, value led organisations who would like to take this proven model to their communities and help us to scale it, and impact adults with intellectual disabilities and neuro divergent adults nationwide.” </span>

<strong>Training</strong>

<span>She also spoke about the accessible communications training provided by Connections Arts Centre. </span>

<span>“From June 28 of this year the European Accessibility Act requires that many organisations in the EU make their products, services and information accessible to all. This includes ensuring that your website, your documents, your customer communications and digital content are perceivable, understandable and usable by everyone. As someone who is dyslexic with ADHD I urge you to do this. It would help so many people. </span>

<span>“Our accessible communications that we have designed and our training programme will give your team skills to meet their legal obligations while improving your customer experience and your reputation and strengthening inclusion across your organisation. There are a lot of people in the room who need this training so please think of us, a social enterprise. Help us to grow our earned income and to continue to do the work that we do.”</span>

&nbsp;

<strong>* More information on Connections Arts Centre is available through its website </strong><a href="http://www.connectionsartscentre.ie"><span>www.connectionsartscentre.ie</span></a>

&nbsp;', 'Some of the value of the Social Inclusion and Community Activation Programme (SICAP) for people with disabilities was outlined by Miriam Spollen, Founder and CEO of Connections Arts Centre.', '/files/uploads/2025/11/324A4280.webp', '[{"slug":"funding-programmes","title":"Funding Programmes"}]', '[{"slug":"connections-arts-centre","title":"Connections Arts Centre"},{"slug":"miriam-spollen","title":"Miriam Spollen"},{"slug":"sicap-social-inclusion-community-activation-programme","title":"SICAP"}]', 'Owen Ryan', 255689, 0, datetime('now'), datetime('now'))
