VALUES ('Revitalisation of Clonfanlough Hall rewarded', 'post-255738', '&nbsp;

<span>However neither are inevitable and the success of Clonfanlough Hall Committee in turning the tide was recognised at the Pride of Place awards, when they took a Special Award at the recent IPB Pride of Place awards. </span>

<span>Speaking about the area, Clonfanlough Hall Committee Chair Lorraine Guinan said, “This is a small rural area in Offaly. We’re about 3km from Clonmacnoise, the historical site. We would have a small population, an ageing population and we would be a peatlands community. We are surrounded by bog here. The hall is 75 years here and it’s the only thing that we have in the community, that and the church.”</span>

<span>Not so long ago the Hall was in decline, but that has been reversed quite dramatically. “During Covid it kind of closed, and the existing committee fell away. Then in September 2023 we put a new committee in place, there are 11 of us on it. </span>

<span>“We had two main aims, one was to bring activities and the community back into the hall and get it used again, and to try and have something for every age group. The second aim was to try and avail of the grants available to do renovation works. While it had been maintained by the previous committee and locals doing bits and bobs on it, it needed a lot of work. The roof was leaking, the stage was starting to come away from the back wall. So the second aim was to apply for grants, which we did successfully.”</span>

<span>With funding secured work on its renewal began in September and is continuing. </span>

<strong>Energy</strong>

<span>Lorraine said that judges were impressed with the amount of progress that has been made in a relatively short time.</span>

<span>She also feels the new committee has hit the ground running.</span>

<span>“There is a lot of energy, a lot of willingness to work. Everyone has played a part, it’s not just two or three. Everyone has done something, organised a fundraiser or worked on a grant application or come and done teas and coffees at events. They all want to see the hall being used for the benefit of the community and really want to see the work being done to make sure the hall is there for the next generation.”</span>

<span>If the building had been neglected for a while more it may have been too late, she added.</span>

<span>“As I mentioned the roof was starting to leak and it’s an asbestos roof. Certainly if the work wasn’t done in the next five years the Hall would have to be closed up. It would have just gone.”</span>

<strong>Activity</strong>

<span>The Community Centre is now very well utilised. “For the younger kids we had an arts and crafts club last winter and that was run by a staff member at a local Montessori. There’s the Youth Club, which is for kids aged from 8-12, they’re in the Hall every Friday night and they do a range of different activities. It’s run by the parents of the kids. </span>

<span>“We have started back social badminton for over 18s, it would have been huge in the Hall going back years ago. There has been a great response to it. There is Pilates on a Monday night, Offaly Music Generation do music lessons on a Wednesday evening for kids aged over eight. It’s the first time we have ever had music lessons in the area. </span>

<span>“Then we have the lunch club, which is for Over 55s. It’s on once a month, Offaly Local Development Company come in and do it, which is amazing, because all of us on the committee are volunteers and are working. They come in and do a dinner and tea and dancing, for anyone who is over the age of 55. That’s always well attended. </span>

<span>“There’d be other events throughout the year, we’ve had two Christmas markets, we had a tractor run and we’re doing an all-vehicle light up run on December 27, to try and raise more money.”</span>

<span>While Lorraine was pleased with the direction of travel, she didn’t expect to get the Hall Committee to get the recognition that it has. </span>

<span>“We were extremely surprised to get the call from Offaly County Council about being nominated first of all. With Clonfanlough being so small we never thought we’d be asked to represent Offaly. It was a great achievement and a great honour to represent Offaly in Pride of Place, especially when you saw the other groups that were there. I</span><span>t was brilliant to be able to bring something home from it.”</span>

&nbsp;', 'Dereliction and decay are constant risks in lightly populated parts of rural Ireland.', '/files/uploads/2025/11/Clonfanlough-Hall.jpg', '[{"slug":"community","title":"Community"}]', '[{"slug":"clonfanlough","title":"Clonfanlough"},{"slug":"offaly","title":"Offaly"},{"slug":"pride-of-place","title":"Pride of Place"}]', 'Owen Ryan', 255738, 0, datetime('now'), datetime('now'))
