VALUES ('Tracton work on biodiversity honoured', 'post-255742', '&nbsp;

<span>A spokesperson said the Group are hugely honoured by the recognition.</span>

<span>“This Pride of Place award validates something we know in our hearts: that ordinary </span><span>people, working together with knowledge, passion, and persistence and guided by a </span><span>love of the natural world can make an extraordinary difference. </span>

<span>“It recognizes that taking action on climate change and biodiversity conservation starts with reconnecting communities with their natural heritage and ancient wisdom, having conversations about what is precious to us and building relationships and local </span><span>knowledge and expertise in order to make effective and lasting change.”</span>

<span>The group said that many people have contributed to the success. </span><span>“This award belongs to every member who joined our walks, every specialist who shared their knowledge, every landowner who welcomed us onto their land or joined our walks and seminars, every community member who came to an event or shared our message, every artist who gave their time or contributed their creative artworks expressing their connection to the natural world.</span>

<span>“We are deeply honoured, tremendously proud, and more committed than ever to protecting the natural heritage of Tracton for generations to come.”</span>

<b>Achievements</b>

<span>The group said it has a lot of successes in promoting local conservation.</span>

<span>“Tracton Biodiversity Group’s activities combine scientific data collection with inclusive community engagement. Using GIS technology and the expertise of our members alongside external specialists, we identified and documented 22 sites of extraordinary biodiversity value—places of such biodiversity value that our findings can now inform Cork County Council’s conservation planning.</span>

<span>“Through monthly biodiversity outings led by various specialists, our members have </span><span>made remarkable discoveries: the rare and protected Borrer’s Salt Marsh Grass at </span><span>Minane Marsh, Toothwort—a rare flowering plant indicating ancient woodland—and the</span><span> native Irish Whitebeam (Sorbus Hibernica), which we’ve since propagated and </span><span>replanted to ensure its survival. We’ve documented both the Dark Bush Cricket and </span><span>Oak Bush Cricket and the beautiful protected Smooth Newt (Eas Luachra), and </span><span>identified protected Tuffa grass and petrified Tuffa Springs on Ringabella Beach. </span><span>Each discovery underscores the richness all around us.”</span>

<span>In 2020 the group planted 5,500 native trees. Its engagement with Coillte secured Bioclass 3 classification for a local woodland, which resulted in the replanting of a previously clear-felled section. It commissioned a survey of a local river that showed the presence of salmon, trout and European eel-species that require pristine water quality. The group also worked with Cork County Council to regenerate habitat at a local quarry following roadworks. </span>

<span>However the Group says its biggest achievement is creating a community around biodiversity.</span>

<span>“Our four annual heritage weekend events since 2022 have drawn packed audiences to inspiring talks, art exhibitions by local artists, farm walks, and nature-based workshops. Our 2023 Agroforestry conference brought farmers together to explore sustainable land management. The launch of our community-led booklet ‘Waters, People and Precious Places’ and our videos celebrating Tracton’s special habitats have reached hundreds of people across our growing network.</span>

<span>“Through collaborations with organizations like BSBI, Cork Sub Aqua Club (who presented their seagrass research), and others, and ongoing citizen science projects with UCC, we’ve created a web of many partnerships that amplifies our impact beyond Tracton’s boundaries.”</span>', 'Tracton Biodiversity Group were winners of the Climate Action & Biodiversity award at the Pride of Place Awards.', '/files/uploads/2025/11/Rodney-Daunt-and-Helen-ODowd-Tracton-Biodiversity-Grouop-scaled.jpg', '[{"slug":"community","title":"Community"}]', '[{"slug":"biodiversity","title":"Biodiversity"},{"slug":"cork","title":"Cork"},{"slug":"tracton","title":"Tracton"}]', 'Owen Ryan', 255742, 0, datetime('now'), datetime('now'))
