VALUES ('Back 2 Boston bringing marathon benefits to Limerick', 'post-255760', '<span>At 35 years of age John Kinsella began a life changing journey when he embraced running.</span>

<span>In just seven weeks he lost four stone, and he would go on to represent Ireland.</span>

<span>From Southill, he says he grew up seeing the State pathologist on the streets around his home and then on TV, and he is passionate about bringing the benefits of running to people living in the most disadvantaged areas of Limerick.</span>

<span>John is now aged 45 and is running the Back 2 Boston project, through which he trains people and gives them an opportunity to run in the Boston marathon. </span>

<span>On the project’s website it says “Our mission is to empower individuals from disadvantaged areas by providing them with the opportunity to run a marathon and ultimately guide runners to qualify and hopefully participate in a future Boston Marathon. Through comprehensive training, mentorship, and support, we aim to demonstrate how running can transform lives, foster personal growth, and build a sense of community.”</span>

<strong>Discipline and benefits</strong>

<span>Marathon running requires an intense level of organisation and dedication, but the pay offs are also huge.</span>

<span>Many find the greater focus and discipline spill into other areas of life, while there are significant mental and physical health benefits from the training. Eventually there is a significant feeling of accomplishment at having run 26.2 miles after months of training.</span>

<span>John says it changed everything for him, and he wants other people to feel the same benefits. </span>

<span>“I ended up running for Ireland. I realised I was very lucky, because a lot of people I grew up with are not here any more.</span>

<span>“I was 35 when I found out I had this gift for running and I said I wasn’t going to waste it; I’ve been pushing myself ever since. I’ve represented my country and I wanted to show people in these areas that you can achieve a lot if you get a chance, and having the chance is the thing. </span>

<span>“I was very lucky I was good at something and I could have been one of the people that are not here today, the way my life was when I was growing up. If I see ten kids playing soccer in Moyross or Southill and I know for a fact that a lot of them are going to have troubles in their lives growing up, even though they are just young, innocent kids. It’s something I don’t like but all I can do is give back through running and try to get people to believe in themselves that way.”</span>

<span>Of course there are a lot of marathons closer to home, but there are longstanding links between the Boston marathon and Southill, and the carrot of a trip to the US helps.</span>

<span>“I grew up in Southill and lads from Southill went to Boston back in the 80s and that was a link I wanted to reinvigorate. Just to have a pathway there, for people to think ‘if I can run there’s a chance I can go to Boston’. That’s the way I wanted it to be. Because I grew up in Southill I knew most of the lads there and it was easy to find interest there.”</span>

<strong>Transformative</strong>

<span>Several of those involved are in recovery and John says it''s very important that people with few options get the opportunity to access something that can be hugely transformative. </span>

<span>“I want to help them because they’re the people that can’t pay for coaching, that need the help and want the help.”</span>

<span>The group meet at UL every week, and he has found it tough to put Back to Boston on a firm footing, with very little, if any, support available. </span>

<span>“We have no base or anything. I had this in my head for about three years, I was talking to politicians but not one of them did anything for me, I had to do everything myself. I designed the website, I paid for it, I hosted it. I bought jerseys, put logos on them, and then I’m coaching the lads while I’m trying to do everything else. It’s only recently I got some funding, got a bit of gear out of it, but until that there were doors being closed all over the place.”</span>

<span>The group has had an unforgettable 2025, as 19 year old Ava Crean won the Dublin City Marathon in the Back to Boston colours. </span>

<span>Ava is not from a disadvantaged background herself, but John says her example is huge for the other members. “She’s a role model and I’d love to help her even more by getting her sponsorship and things like that. If I got the project moving along I’d get her involved in coaching and stuff, things that would keep her around the people who want help.”</span>

&nbsp;

<strong>More information is available on <a href="http://www.back2boston.ie">www.back2boston.ie</a>. </strong>

&nbsp;', 'At 35 years of age John Kinsella began a life changing journey when he embraced running. In just seven weeks he lost four stone, and he would go on to represent Ireland.', '/files/uploads/2025/11/Back-2-Boston-group.jpg', '[{"slug":"community","title":"Community"}]', '[{"slug":"back-2-boston","title":"Back 2 Boston"},{"slug":"john-kinsella","title":"John Kinsella"},{"slug":"limerick","title":"Limerick"},{"slug":"southill","title":"Southill"}]', 'Owen Ryan', 255760, 0, datetime('now'), datetime('now'))
