INSERT INTO posts (title, slug, content, excerpt, image_url, categories, tags, author, wp_id, published, created_at, updated_at)
VALUES ('All-party support to stop “privatisation”, yet gov’t presses on', 'allparty-support-to-stop-privatisation-yet-govt-presses-on', 'Amidst a nationwide campaign to save <strong>Local Employment Services and Jobs Clubs</strong>, and in the face of notable Dáil opposition, <strong>Minister Heather Humphreys</strong> continued in late November to insist that the attorney general’s advice meant the Department of Social Protection was obliged to put these services out to public procurement.
The campaign against creeping privatisation has been led by community workers, trade unions and local development companies, among others - and the <strong>Irish Local Development Network</strong> has presented the Minister with an alternative reading of EU law on the matter.
As <strong>Claire Kerrane (Sinn Féin)</strong> argued in the Dáil on November 30, “The same EU directive is crystal clear in stating that no member state is obliged ‘to contract out...the provision of services that they wish to provide themselves or to organise by means other than public contracts’.”

Kerrane is Sinn Fein’s spokesperson for Social Protection and Rural Development.

<a href="https://twitter.com/ClaireKerrane" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/files/uploads/2021/12/Claire-Kerrane-TD-putting-forward-her-motion-in-the-Dáil-on-Nov-30.jpg" alt="" width="679" height="392" /></a> <em>• Claire Kerrane TD putting forward her motion in the Dáil on Nov 30th.</em>
<h3>"Extremely significant", says SIPTU</h3>
Unusually, the motion she proposed in the Dáil on November 30th passed without opposition.
<strong>SIPTU</strong> said this was “an extremely significant milestone in the #OurCommunityisNotforSale campaign’.
The motion called on the Government to “protect the not-for-profit and community-based ethos of employment services and... to suspend all plans to tender out employment services.”
Earlier, outside the Dáil, staff from Local Employment Services and Job Clubs and ILDN members had protested and called on TDs to “Keep Our Doors Open”.
Yet the Government is pressing on, which means that community-based, not-for-profit organisations face closure, while profiteering companies stand to benefit at a measurable cost to communities; on this TDs are all but united.

<strong>Rose Conway-Walsh (SF)</strong> worked for many years co-ordinating a community development project in West Mayo. During the debate, she described the Government’s proposal as a mistake and compared it with “detrimental changes” made in the past to home care supports.

<strong>Labour TD Marie Sherlock</strong> gave an example from Dublin’s Docklands where the local LES enables people who otherwise would not get a chance to earn a living. “Some have been in prison, some have had addiction issues,” she said. She condemned “this crusade to dismantle local employment services”.

https://twitter.com/marie_sherlock/status/1465635551445458948

<strong>Sinn Fein TD Sorca Clarke</strong> repeated what many other deputies said - that the system didn’t need fixing - but more support. Others spoke about the importance of the “wrap-around service” provided by community-based, not-for-profits.

In recent months, people across the country have testified about Local Employment Services and Job Clubs, saying how the unique supports they provided change lives. Trade unions have called on the Minister “not to organise social services for profit”.
On September 29th, worker representatives appeared before the Joint-Oireachtas Committee for Social Protection to outline their concerns. They won all-party support for the retention of the existing service. This was highlighted by <strong>Galway TD Catherine Connolly (Ind) and Cork’s Thomas Gould (SF)</strong> among others on November 30th.
<h3>Against our ethos say workers facing axe</h3>
Overall, there are 26 LESs and 40 Job Clubs in existence, employing a total workforce of 388 full-time equivalents.
LESs have been working at community level across the State, particularly in disadvantaged areas, for 25 years.
<strong>Samantha O’Toole from Galway LES</strong> is among those likely to lose her job and she <a href="https://www.rte.ie/news/ireland/2021/0929/1249560-employment-oireachtas-committeee/">recently told RTE</a> it was against their ethos to want “to want to make a profit from people”.
“People shouldn’t be commodities that we can actually make money from,” she said.

<img src="/files/uploads/2021/12/Samantha-OToole-Galway-LES-speaks-to-RTE-1.png" alt="" width="1151" height="649" /> <em>• Samantha O''Toole, Galway LES, speaks to RTE.</em>
<h3>Minister''s Reply</h3>
Replying to the motion, <strong>Minister Humphreys</strong> complimented the work of LESs, but added:
“What I cannot escape, however, is the clear legal advice from the Attorney General that these services must be subject to a competitive procurement process. Representatives of the ILDN have written to me to state that it has legal advice to say that there is no legal requirement to tender. The legal advice from the ILDN was not shared with me or my officials. Instead, the organisation provided a high-level synopsis of the legal opinion in a letter to me. This was shared with the Office of the Attorney General and its view is that its original advice still stands: there must be an open procurement process. I must follow the legal advice of the Attorney General, and whether the Government likes it, we have no choice but to put these contracts out to tender.”
<h3>Disappointed</h3>
<strong>Deputy Kerrane</strong>, having proposed the motion, concluded by pointing out that JobPath cost €275m, had a success rate of only 7% and now more of the same was promised.
“How is 7% a success?” she asked.
“I will conclude by saying that the cross-party committee, including Fianna Fáil, Fine Gael and Green Party members of the committee, put forward the report, with no objection, stating that the job clubs and local employment services should remain as they are,” she said.
She said it was “a joke that the Government will support this motion, which calls for suspending the tendering, when it knows it is not going to do that.”
The motion did indeed pass, which Kerrane afterwards described as “incredible”.
<strong>The full debate is on the Oireachtas website via this link:</strong>
<a href="https://bit.ly/OIR-LES-Nov30">https://bit.ly/OIR-LES-Nov30</a>

https://changingireland.ie/our-communities-are-not-for-sale/

https://changingireland.ie/community-based-jobs-service-may-lose-out-in-tendering/

https://changingireland.ie/call-to-protect-community-based-local-employment-services/

https://changingireland.ie/local-employment-service-les-deasp-research/', 'This is against our ethos, say Local Employment Service workers facing axe, as the Dáil passes Sinn Féin motion supporting them and the service they provide for unemployed people. Unions described the passing of the motion as “an extremely significant milestone" in the #OurCommunityisNotforSale campaign.', '/files/uploads/2021/12/Protesters-outside-the-Dail-on-November-30th-photo-courtesy-ILDN.jpg', '[{"slug":"editors-picks","title":"Editor''s Picks"},{"slug":"inclusion-editors-choice","title":"Inclusion (Editor’s Choice)"}]', '[{"slug":"ourcommunityisnotforsale","title":"#OurCommunityisNotforSale"},{"slug":"claire-kerrane","title":"Claire Kerrane"},{"slug":"jobs-clubs","title":"Jobs Clubs"},{"slug":"local-employment-service","title":"Local Employment Service"},{"slug":"sinn-fein","title":"Sinn Féin"},{"slug":"siptu","title":"siptu"}]', 'Allen Meagher', 251331, 0, '2021-12-02T10:23:58Z', '2021-12-02T10:23:58Z')
