INSERT INTO posts (title, slug, content, excerpt, image_url, categories, tags, author, wp_id, published, created_at, updated_at)
VALUES ('“People were seeing the positivity within the area,”', 'post-255793', '&nbsp;

<span>“Our success comes from the commitment we have to the group and the support we offer to each other,” said Jacqueline O’Brien Langan, chair of Ballyfermot Sister Shed and also project co-ordinator for Sister Sheds Ireland.</span>

<span>“We have 25 sheds and we started the Ballyfermot one four years ago. We’re in the Cherry Orchard area and we have between 30 and 35 members. We have had an awful lot of personal development courses that have led to a boost in the confidence of members and given them the ability to go on in some cases to further education, some have gone back to the workplace. It was set up to diminish isolation in the community, after Covid a lot of people were very apprehensive about returning into community areas. </span>

<span>“We were very successful and very quickly we had to create a second shed in the Ballyfermot area called the Kylemore/Chapelizod shed. People were seeing the positivity within the area around what we had created,” she added. </span>

<span>In general Sister Sheds are proving very popular across Ireland, although she says they have to try and expand without State backing. </span>

<span>“ We have waiting lists in areas all over the country. The unfortunate thing is we are not funded by Government. We are a registered charity and we are fighting for funding. We have two sponsors over two years in Elavon and Fidelis insurance which have been very helpful, but most of the sheds and their activities are funded by the members. We pay a membership of €42.50 to the CLG and this covers insurance and other fees for the year. Most of us put €5 a week into a kitty too and we avail of any funding opportunities that may arise to do the courses that the women are interested in. The ones that funding isn’t available for that we want to do; we let our kitty build up and try and get as much bang for our buck as we can.”</span>

<span>There is a special needs Shed in Tallaght, and the organisation wants to include everyone that it can, as much as is possible. </span>

<span>Jacqueline says that a key focus is promoting mental health of members and their families and friends.</span>

<span>“We are all about supporting mental health in the community, giving people the tools to support their own mental health if they feel that it is dipping. It’s not just what we’re all about, supporting each other is what we’re all about, but mental health does affect everyone in the community.”</span>

&nbsp;', 'Ballyfermot Sister Shed has had to expand, such is the demand for what it provides, and it was a winner of a special award at the IPB Pride of Place awards.', '/files/uploads/2025/11/sister-shed-craft-activity-scaled.jpeg', '[{"slug":"community","title":"Community"}]', '[{"slug":"award","title":"award"},{"slug":"ballyfermot","title":"Ballyfermot"},{"slug":"dublin","title":"Dublin"},{"slug":"pride-of-place","title":"Pride of Place"},{"slug":"sister-sheds","title":"Sister Sheds"}]', 'Owen Ryan', 255793, 0, datetime('now'), datetime('now'))
