INSERT INTO posts (title, slug, content, excerpt, image_url, categories, tags, author, wp_id, published, created_at, updated_at)
VALUES ('Calls to end orphanage volunteering for children’s sake', 'post-255806', '<span> </span>

<span>While that organisation is leading a campaign to end the practice, Liam Grant, Chief Executive Officer of Chernobyl Aid Ireland, feels the position it has adopted may be too simplistic, and doesn’t take cognisance of what might happen to those being housed in orphanages if such facilities weren’t operating. </span>

<span>“If the orphanages weren’t there, what would the kids do? Would they be on the street? It depends on the country. If the orphanages weren’t there where would the kids go? There’s no such thing as foster homes, people are so poor there that they can’t feed themselves,” he said.</span>

<span>He set up Chernobyl Aid Ireland in 1997 to help an orphanage 100km outside of Minsk called Grozovo, which was then in serious need of refurbishment. Since then it has been extensively renovated, due to funding and work from Irish people. </span>

<span>Mr Grant doesn’t feel that support to such places needs to end. “I probably wouldn’t agree with that. Now I would only have been dealing with one orphanage, but the kids were very well cared for all the way through until they went to foster homes.”</span>

<span>He has faith in the facility at Grozovo. “Now I would only have been dealing with one orphanage, but the kids were very well cared for all the way through until they went to foster homes. </span>

<span>The Burren Chernobyl Project, which works with two orphanages in Eastern Europe, was unavailable for comment.</span>

<span>On its website Cork based Chernobyl Children International says that it is working towards ending reliance on institutions.</span>

<span>“In the Chernobyl regions there are hundreds of orphanages and institutions where thousands of children live lonely forgotten lives. Chernobyl Children International works to support children in institutions across Belarus to regain their right to a family life and to end the institutionalisation of children in conjunction with Belarusian authorities.”</span>

<b>80% in orphanages have living parent</b>

<span>Comhlámh say that out of 5.4 million children in institutions around the world, more than 80% have at least one living parent. It says that poverty and lack of access to health care and education is a key driver of institutionalisation along with social marginalisation and discrimination in this statistic, while people volunteering is another reason</span>

<span>“Although overwhelmingly well-intentioned orphanage volunteering is also a key driver of this institutionalisation. Orphanages are being created to meet the demand from well-meaning tourists, volunteers, and donors, fuelling the trafficking of children and perpetuating what is now termed an ‘orphanage industry’.”</span>

<span>On the negative impact of volunteering, it adds. “Orphanage volunteering also contributes to the harmful effects of institutions. The general transient nature of volunteering can intensify children’s feelings of abandonment and loss and most volunteers do not have the required skills to meet the needs of very vulnerable children already traumatised by the separation from their families. Volunteering in orphanages also presents key child protection concerns as most institutions are not state regulated and do not have adequate safeguarding systems, exposing children to significant risks of abuse.”</span>

<b>Policy</b>

<span>It is looking four policy changes in Ireland</span>

<span>*It asks that Irish Aid introduce a dedicated funding stream for care reform strategies.</span>

<span>* The Department of Foreign Affairs introduce foreign travel advice warning of the harm caused by orphanage volunteering and that it encourage people not to visit or volunteer.</span>

<span>*it asks that Irish Aid introduce funding criteria that no programmes or activities that involve sending volunteers to orphanages be supported.</span>

<span>*It also asks that relevant Government departments “develop child safe guidelines for trips overseas, including guidance not to visit or to volunteer in orphanages.”</span>

<span>Comhlámh has warned that there is a link between orphanage volunteering and orphanage trafficking. “Orphanage trafficking involves the active recruitment of children into institutions for the purpose of financial and other forms of exploitation. Research by Lumos has found that some so-called ‘orphanages’ in countries such as Haiti, Cambodia, Nepal and Uganda are set up as profit-making operations to extract funding from volunteers and donors. Orphanage volunteering and foreign donor interest feed this lucrative ‘orphanage industry’.”</span>

<span>It said there is a huge inconsistency in Irish people without relevant skills going to work in foreign institutions, when they would not be permitted to do so here. </span>

<span>“Many people wish to support children at risk, including children in the Global South. However, we wouldn’t permit large scale access of unskilled short-term volunteers to vulnerable children on this island. We need to apply the same standards universally and work to protect and ensure the welfare of all children.”</span>

<b>Young people volunteering</b>

<span>It should be made clear to schools and youth groups that on foreign visits Irish young people shouldn’t volunteer or visit at orphanages, it claims. “An aspect of these trips abroad often includes a visit or volunteering experience to an orphanage that the school sponsors or supports. These trips contribute to the regular flow of volunteers, visitors and donations to institutional systems which work to separate children from their families, harms a child’s development and exposes them to increased risk of abuse and exploitation. They also contribute to the growing global orphanage industry, dependent on the recruitment and trafficking of children into institutions. We need to be child safe and ensure school and youth trips don’t help perpetuate systems which separate children from their families and expose them to further risks.”</span>

&nbsp;', 'The generosity of spirit that leads Irish people to volunteer in orphanages is misguided, and it would be far better if people stopped going to them from Ireland, according to Comhlámh, the national Association of International Development Workers and Volunteers.', '/files/uploads/2025/12/comhlamh-volunteer-campaigning-against-orphanage-volunteering.jpg', '[{"slug":"development-work","title":"Development Work"}]', '[{"slug":"comhlamh","title":"Comhlamh"},{"slug":"orphanage","title":"orphanage"},{"slug":"volunteering","title":"volunteering"}]', 'Owen Ryan', 255806, 0, datetime('now'), datetime('now'))
