VALUES ('“I don’t think it’s a space we should fear”', 'post-255855', '&nbsp;

<span>It is looking to set up an approved housing body, which will provide housing to older people and certain disadvantaged groups in its catchment area.</span>

<span>Terry Hyland is the development company’s CEO and he said that it is still in the initial stages, but it has set a direction of travel.</span>

<span>“We are in the very early stages of it yet, we are in the process of setting up a company to look to be an approved housing body. The way we look at it, we provide services to the most disadvantaged in our community, why couldn’t we provide a roof over their heads as well? The company hasn’t been set up yet, but it was approved by the board back at our September meeting. Early in the New Year we’ll be looking at ramping it up.”</span>

<span>On who it will look to provide housing for, he said,</span>

<span>“Our target groups will probably be over 55s and disadvantaged cohorts such as people with disabilities or young people coming out of care, those will probably be our three focuses. Our plan is to work with Cavan County Council and to take those cohorts off the social housing list. Also working with Tusla as well.”</span>

<strong>Options</strong>

<span>Deciding to move into property development isn’t necessarily very simple or easy, and Cavan County Local Development have been looking at their options for some time.</span>

<span>“We have done a lot of research into it. We have met with some established approved housing bodies as well around the country. We met with the approved housing body regulator, we have got a lot of advice from the Irish Council for Social Housing which is a representative network for approved housing bodies.”</span>

<span>He feels that there is an obvious link between what CCLD does and what providing housing would mean. </span>

<span>“It’s in our constitution to tackle poverty, to promote social inclusion. If we were providing housing to the people that we are supporting on a daily basis we would really be providing a wraparound support service for those people. That’s the model we are looking at. A lot of approved housing bodies out there can provide a roof over people’s heads, but they probably don’t have the capacity to provide services, whereas we can provide programmes like SICAP, Healthy Communities and Tus, all of these other programmes that we deliver. We won’t just provide a roof, we’ll be able to cater for their economic and social needs as well.”</span>

<span>While local development companies going into the provision of housing is a new idea, there are very suitable options available in terms of getting the substantial funding that is required. </span>

<span>“There is a fund through local authorities and Tusla where you can get 100% funding for purchase of built properties. It won’t cost us anything. Essentially what you are doing is taking out a 30 year mortgage but it is backed by Tusla or the local authority, it’s called the Capital Assistance Scheme. There are a lot of other funding models for approved housing bodies, but this is the least risky one.</span>

<span>“It’s a non repayable loan to approved housing bodies that provide housing for specific priority groups such as elderly, homeless or people with disabilities. Some of the larger approved housing bodies have other funding models, but that’s probably the least risky one starting off, if you breach your contract you have to repay the loan, but if you don’t breach your contract you never have to repay it.”</span>

<strong>Follow the leader?</strong>

<span>Does he feel that local development companies generally will follow the Cavan example?</span>

<span>“They might, or they might just wait to see how we get on. We might just be the guinea pig! I don’t think it’s a space we should fear. We deal a lot with Government departments and funders, we’re good at project managing funding. We’re used to handling large sums of taxpayers money and providing value for money. It’s a space we definitely could look at.”</span>

<span>He feels that local development companies would actually be far more capable of delivering housing than many small organisations that have already done so. </span>

<span>“A lot of the smaller approved housing bodies are very volunteer-led. They don’t employ staff. There’s a lot of regulation, governance and compliance that goes with being an approved housing body, but a local development company already has or should have resources to be able to meet that compliance and regulatory need. </span>

<span>“What you might find coming down the tracks is that the volunteer-led approved housing bodies, I’m talking about the smaller ones where you might have half a dozen people in a small town or village; they might be struggling to be sustainable into the future, because of the compliance and checks that are there for them. They’re obviously volunteers and there are time constraints. You might find a lot of those handing over their properties to a larger entity. When a lot of people think of approved housing bodies they think of Respond or Focus, those bigger organisations. But there are hundreds of approved housing bodies throughout the country and a lot of them are very small, community, volunteer led organisations and they are finding it tough.”</span>

<span>He is very much of the view that providing housing would be a suitable extension of the work already being done, and he is keen to make progress, without moving too fast. </span>

<span>“We work with the most disadvantaged in society and there is a housing need. There is a lot of rhetoric of new communities taking housing from Irish people and that kind of stuff and we just think ‘let’s try and be part of the solution here if we can’. We are set up to alleviate poverty, to promote social inclusion, so it does fall in line with the ethos of what we do. That’s why we think we could be well placed to do it and local development companies could be well placed to do it nationally. We’ll take a step into the unknown in terms of moving from service delivery to having portfolios of property. We’ll dip our toe into it, we won’t walk before we can run. We will build capacity gradually.”</span>

&nbsp;', 'Housing is surely one of the defining issues of the time, and Cavan County Local Development is going to do what it can to help.', '/files/uploads/2025/12/Terry-Hyland.avif', '[{"slug":"development-work","title":"Development Work"}]', '[{"slug":"approved-housing-body","title":"approved housing body"},{"slug":"cavan","title":"Cavan"},{"slug":"cavan-county-local-development","title":"Cavan County Local Development"},{"slug":"ccld","title":"CCLD"},{"slug":"housing","title":"housing"}]', 'Owen Ryan', 255855, 0, datetime('now'), datetime('now'))
