INSERT INTO posts (title, slug, content, excerpt, image_url, categories, tags, author, wp_id, published, created_at, updated_at)
VALUES ('Opening up book clubs to the male of the species', 'post-257708', '<span>Book clubs are a social and community outlet available in pretty much every town and village throughout the country.</span>

<span>Yet, for various reasons, the make up of the clubs is almost always predominatly or exclusively female. </span>

<span>With male loneliness being a complex and indeed growing issue, expanding outlets for male community participation is very welcome, and it''s something that is happening in two book clubs, almost 300 miles apart.</span>

<span>Library assistant Paul Murphy was founding the Yorke Book Club down south in 2024, around the same time as Cillian McGivern was establishing the Belfast chapter of the Tough Guy book club. </span>

<span>The names reflect the need to assure the participants of the very masculinity of the groups, and that it is a space for them. While the Tough Guy reference is obvious, younger readers may not recall the marketing phrase "It''s Not For Girls" used to promote Yorkie from 2002 to 2011.</span>

<strong>Something new</strong>

<span>"We noticed there were a few book clubs that were operating in the library, and there were all women in them. We decided it''d be good to have a space for men who want to join a book club, because they''re not going to join one if it''s all women in the group realistically," says Paul.</span>

<span>With a male colleague he discussed the possibility of forming a male book club and decided to go for it.</span>

<span>"We put the word out, we were telling all the men who''d be coming in that there''d be a men''s book club, so we kind of had a feeling that there''d be a few people anyway. If you don''t try something you''ll never know if it''s going to work.</span>

<span>"We had a small group at the first one, we were just telling people how it works and looking to find people who were interested, and taking it from there. It has kind of developed from that and now we get feedback about what kind of books to pick, what people like and don''t like, we try to have a variety of stuff."</span>

<strong>Expanding the range of reading</strong>

<span>Being in a book club obviously appeals to regular readers, and one of the benefits is that it can take them out of their literary comfort zone. </span>

<span>"We try to have a bit of variety, we might pick one type of book this month and next month would be a different type. People say they enjoy the fact that they are reading books they wouldn''t normally read. A lot of people read the same types of books all the time, but in a book club you read different ones, ones you wouldn''t normally pick up."</span>

<span>The men attending are enjoying the monthly meet up, he adds.</span>

<span>"They say it''s good to have a social outlet that doesn''t involve going to the pub. A lot of people have an interest in books but don''t have a place to go and talk about books to other people. They find it''s good to be reading different types of books to what they normally read."</span>

<span>Why does he feel that men are unlikely to join book clubs? "Similarly why are women more likely to work in a library? I suppose it''s seen as a thing that women would do moreso. Why that is, I don''t know, it''s one of those things," says Paul</span>

<span>He believes that the example could be followed in other parts of Ireland. </span>

<span>"I''m not sure it''s something that could work everywhere, but definitely in some of the bigger libraries around the country it''s something they could bring in."</span>

<strong>From Australia to Belfast</strong>

<span>The Tough Guy Book Club is primarily an Australian phenomenon, with a small number of other chapters around the world. </span>

<span>At the moment there is just one in Ireland, in Belfast, and it was founded by Cillian McGivern, whose day job is a community development worker with Belfast City Council.</span>

<span>He came across the Tough Guy Book Club on the other side of the world.</span>

<span>"I lived in Melbourne and I was starting to read recreationally as an adult for the first time. I googled something like what books should men under 30 read. That prompted Google to adverstise the Tough Guy book club to me, it was around the corner from where I was living. Being an immigrant in Australia you can be somewhat isolated, you are away from your community, but it helped scratch the itch for me, having some community, a bit of structure and routine. Because of the diversity of it, you get to chat to people in a somewhat serious, heavy way. These are people you''ll have almost nothing in common with, you''re talking people from different cultures, classes, religions, ages; all different parts of the city."</span>

<span>On the format of the club, he says, "The Tough Guy book club HQ will pick a book. At our meeting we are told what it is and it''s up to people to source a book and to go and read it over the next four weeks and when we come together again HQ will have sent out a list of questions, essentially conversational props that I''ll read out to the room, on the book that we have all just read. Then at the end we are told what the next book is, go and read it, and we''ll chat about it next month."</span>

<strong>Positive Masculinity</strong>

<span>When he spoke to Changing Ireland Cillian was just after watching the Louis Theroux documentary Inside The Manosphere. </span>

<span>He says he is concious of what people might think of the idea of a male only book club in an era where there is so much concern about toxic elements of masculinity, but he says the Tough Guy Book Club is far more positive.</span>

<span>"It''s about helping social isolation among men and there is a bit of an epidemic of thatgoing on. If you see the books you''ll see they are on themes like emigration, sexual identity, and looking at them in an empathetic and progressive way.</span>

<span>"It''s about bringing people together, people who mightn''t necessarily socialise together otherwise, there are beer breaks and you encourage the small talk then. As well as the book to read there''s a monthly challenge and that challenge will often encourage people to come together in an informal setting to spend time together. Those challenges will often be about health and wellbeing. It could be to offer a skill to your book club, if you''re great at IT you can help someone, if you are strong it could be helping someone dig their back garden. It leads to a sense of community and relationships."</span>

&nbsp;

<em><strong>For more information on the Yorkie Book Club contact 066 7121200 or <a href="mailto:info@kerrylibrary.ie">info@kerrylibrary.ie</a>. </strong></em>

<em><strong>The Belfast chapter of the Tough Guy Book Club meets at 7pm on the first Wednesday of each month at the Watson bar in Belfast city centre. More information is available on <a href="http://www.toughguybookclub.com/belfast_ni">www.toughguybookclub.com/belfast_ni</a>.</strong></em>

&nbsp;', 'Book clubs are a social and community outlet available in pretty much every town and village throughout the country. Yet, for various reasons, the make up of the clubs is almost always predominatly or exclusively female. With male loneliness being a complex and indeed growing issue, expanding outlets for male community participation is very welcome, and it''s something that is happening in two book clubs, almost 300 miles apart. Library assistant Paul Murphy was founding the Yorke Book Club down…', '', '[{"slug":"community","title":"Community"},{"slug":"development-work","title":"Development Work"}]', '[{"slug":"belfast","title":"Belfast"},{"slug":"book-clubs","title":"Book Clubs"},{"slug":"kerry","title":"Kerry"},{"slug":"tough-guys","title":"Tough Guys"},{"slug":"yorkie","title":"Yorkie"}]', 'Owen Ryan', 257708, 0, datetime('now'), datetime('now'))
